  <header class="hero">
    <h1>Everything to know before day one</h1>
    <p class="lede">The payments domain, explained from the ground up for someone crossing over from pharma &#8212; and what the job actually asks of you once you land.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span><b>Anurag Kapur</b></span>
      <span>ZS Associates &#8594; Mastercard</span>
      <span>Senior PM &#8211; Technical, Agentic AI</span>
    </div>
    <figure class="dgw">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 170" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Four-party model: cardholder, merchant, acquirer, Mastercard network, issuer, with response returning">
  <defs>
    <marker id="ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
    </marker>
    <marker id="ag" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--gold)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
    </marker>
  </defs>
  
  <g class="n-plain">
    <rect x="22" y="42" width="118" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="81" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Cardholder</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-plain">
    <rect x="156" y="42" width="100" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="206" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Merchant</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-bank">
    <rect x="272" y="42" width="100" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="322" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Acquirer</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-net">
    <rect x="388" y="42" width="122" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="449" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Mastercard</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-bank">
    <rect x="532" y="42" width="92" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="578" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Issuer</text>
  </g>
  <g class="req anim">
    <line x1="140" y1="65" x2="156" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
    <line x1="256" y1="65" x2="272" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
    <line x1="372" y1="65" x2="388" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
    <line x1="510" y1="65" x2="532" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
  </g>
  <path class="resp anim" d="M578 88 L578 134 L206 134 L206 88" fill="none" marker-end="url(#ag)"/>
  <text class="cap anim" x="392" y="127" text-anchor="middle">approve or decline &#183; real time</text>
</svg></figure>
  </header>

  <section class="ch" id="ch0" data-idx="0">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 0</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="0"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>The One Mental Shift That Makes Everything Click</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c0-0">Before any mechanics, internalise one idea, because almost every confusion about payments dissolves once you have it.</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c0-1">Mastercard is not a bank. It never holds your money, never issues your card, and never decides whether your payment is approved. It is a network — it moves messages between banks, sets the rules everyone follows, and takes a small fee on the volume that flows across it.</div></aside><p data-b="c0-2">In your world at ZS, the platform (ZAIDYN) was where the value lived and where the money was. In payments, the bank on each side holds the money and takes the real financial risk, and the network in the middle is the neutral switch that connects them. Mastercard is that switch.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c0-3">You already know a switch like this. When a patient fills a prescription, the pharmacy doesn’t call the insurer directly. It fires the claim into a clearinghouse / switch (Change Healthcare, RelayHealth), which routes it to the right payer / PBM, gets back an approve-or-deny in seconds, and later reconciles the money. Mastercard is that clearinghouse, but for card payments instead of pharmacy claims. Hold this analogy — we’ll reuse it for the whole transaction flow.</div></aside><p data-b="c0-4">If you keep confusing ‘Mastercard’ with ‘the bank,’ you’ll misread half of what your team says. Mastercard connects banks. That’s the whole identity of the company.</p><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c0-5">Say out loud, in one sentence each: what does Mastercard do, and what does it explicitly NOT do? If you find yourself saying ‘it approves payments’ or ‘it lends money,’ reread this part.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="0">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-0"></div>
    <div id="yn-0"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch1" data-idx="1">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 1</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="1"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>How a Payment Actually Works (The Plumbing)</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c1-0">This is the vocabulary everyone around you will use without explaining it. Get fluent here and standups stop feeling like a foreign language.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c1-1"><span class="num">1.1 </span> The Four-Party Model</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 170" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Four-party model: cardholder, merchant, acquirer, Mastercard network, issuer, with response returning">
  <defs>
    <marker id="ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
    </marker>
    <marker id="ag" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--gold)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
    </marker>
  </defs>
  
  <g class="n-plain">
    <rect x="22" y="42" width="118" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="81" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Cardholder</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-plain">
    <rect x="156" y="42" width="100" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="206" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Merchant</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-bank">
    <rect x="272" y="42" width="100" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="322" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Acquirer</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-net">
    <rect x="388" y="42" width="122" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="449" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Mastercard</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-bank">
    <rect x="532" y="42" width="92" height="46" rx="7"/>
    <text x="578" y="65" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Issuer</text>
  </g>
  <g class="req">
    <line x1="140" y1="65" x2="156" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
    <line x1="256" y1="65" x2="272" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
    <line x1="372" y1="65" x2="388" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
    <line x1="510" y1="65" x2="532" y2="65" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
  </g>
  <path class="resp" d="M578 88 L578 134 L206 134 L206 88" fill="none" marker-end="url(#ag)"/>
  <text class="cap" x="392" y="127" text-anchor="middle">approve or decline &#183; real time</text>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c1-2">Every card transaction has four players plus the network. Learn these five roles cold — they are the grammar of the whole industry.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Player</th><th class="no-ann">What they are and what they do</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c1-3">Cardholder</td><td data-b="c1-4">You, buying the coffee. Holds the card, initiates the payment.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-5">Merchant</td><td data-b="c1-6">The coffee shop. Sells the goods, wants to get paid and avoid fraud.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-7">Issuer</td><td data-b="c1-8">The cardholder’s bank. It issued the card, it holds the money or the credit line, and it takes the credit risk. The issuer is who actually approves or declines.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-9">Acquirer</td><td data-b="c1-10">The merchant’s bank. It processes transactions on the merchant’s behalf and deposits the money into the merchant’s account.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-11">Network (Mastercard)</td><td data-b="c1-12">The switch in the middle. Routes the message from acquirer to issuer, carries the yes/no back, sets the rules, and later coordinates who owes whom. Holds no money, takes no credit risk.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><p data-b="c1-13"><em>The request flows left to right; the approve/decline answer comes back in real time (dashed).</em></p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-14">Map it straight onto pharmacy claim adjudication: cardholder = patient; merchant = pharmacy; issuer = the payer / PBM (holds the funds, decides coverage, bears the cost); acquirer = the pharmacy’s claims processor that submits and gets reimbursed; Mastercard = the switch that routes the claim and enforces the rules. The shapes are identical.</div></aside><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-15">The issuer — not Mastercard — makes the approve/decline decision. This trips up everyone new. Mastercard just carries the question to the issuer and carries the answer back.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c1-16"><span class="num">1.2 </span> The Transaction Lifecycle: Authorization → Clearing → Settlement</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 120" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Authorization, clearing and settlement run on three different clocks">
  <defs><marker id="at" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
    <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></marker></defs>
  
  <g class="n-phase">
    <rect x="20" y="26" width="200" height="60" rx="7"/>
    <text x="120" y="48" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Authorization</text>
    <text class="sub" x="120" y="68" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">milliseconds</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-phase">
    <rect x="250" y="26" width="200" height="60" rx="7"/>
    <text x="350" y="48" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Clearing</text>
    <text class="sub" x="350" y="68" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">end of day</text>
  </g>
  <g class="n-phase">
    <rect x="480" y="26" width="200" height="60" rx="7"/>
    <text x="580" y="48" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Settlement</text>
    <text class="sub" x="580" y="68" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">T+1 to T+2</text>
  </g>
  <line class="ph" x1="220" y1="56" x2="250" y2="56" marker-end="url(#at)"/>
  <line class="ph" x1="450" y1="56" x2="480" y2="56" marker-end="url(#at)"/>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c1-17">A single tap actually triggers three distinct phases that happen at different speeds. Confusing them is the most common rookie mistake.</p><h4 class="sub" data-b="c1-18">Phase <span class="num">1 </span>— Authorization (real-time, milliseconds)</h4><p data-b="c1-19">You tap. The terminal reads your card credentials and sends the transaction to the acquirer. The acquirer routes it through the Mastercard network to your issuer. The issuer checks two things almost instantly: do you have the money / credit, and does this look suspicious? It sends back an approval or a decline code. All of this happens in well under a second, while you’re still standing at the counter.</p><h4 class="sub" data-b="c1-20">Phase <span class="num">2 </span>— Clearing (later that day, in a batch)</h4><p data-b="c1-21">Authorization only reserved the money; it didn’t move it. At the end of the day the merchant batches all its approved transactions and sends them through the acquirer to Mastercard, which routes them to the issuers for final confirmation of the exact amounts. Think of this as the paperwork step — everyone agrees on what actually happened.</p><h4 class="sub" data-b="c1-22">Phase <span class="num">3 </span>— Settlement (the money finally moves)</h4><p data-b="c1-23">Mastercard calculates the net positions — how much each bank owes each other bank across all the day’s transactions — and facilitates the actual money movement. The issuer pays the acquirer (minus the interchange fee), and the acquirer credits the merchant (minus its own markup). This is usually a day or two after the purchase.</p><p data-b="c1-24"><em>Three phases, three different clocks — milliseconds, then end-of-day, then a day or two later.</em></p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-25">This is exactly the split you’ve seen in healthcare claims. The real-time ‘approved / denied’ the pharmacy sees at the counter is authorization. The actual reimbursement dollars landing in the pharmacy’s account weeks later, after remittance and reconciliation, is clearing and settlement. Same two-speed system: an instant yes/no, then a slower money movement.</div></aside><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-26">Authorization is a promise; settlement is the payment. A transaction can be authorized (approved) but later fail to settle, and money movement always lags the approval. Keep the two ideas separate.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c1-27"><span class="num">1.3 </span> The Economics: Who Takes a Cut, and How Mastercard Makes Money</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 190" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Of a 100 dollar purchase the merchant keeps about 97.50; interchange goes to the issuer, a small scheme fee to Mastercard, the rest to the acquirer">
  <text class="wf-t" x="20" y="24">Where $100 goes</text>
  <rect class="wf-bar wf-keep" x="20" y="40" width="480" height="34" rx="4"/>
  <text class="wf-in" x="40" y="58" dominant-baseline="central">Merchant keeps ~$97.50</text>
  <rect class="wf-bar wf-ix" x="504" y="40" width="130" height="34" rx="4"/>
  <text class="wf-in wf-on" x="514" y="58" dominant-baseline="central">~$1.75</text>
  <rect class="wf-bar wf-mc" x="638" y="40" width="26" height="34" rx="4"/>
  <rect class="wf-bar wf-aq" x="668" y="40" width="30" height="34" rx="4"/>
  <g class="wf-leg">
    <rect class="wf-sw wf-ix" x="20" y="100" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/>
    <text x="40" y="106" dominant-baseline="central">Interchange &#8594; the issuer, for carrying the credit risk</text>
    <rect class="wf-sw wf-mc" x="20" y="126" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/>
    <text x="40" y="132" dominant-baseline="central">Scheme fee &#8594; Mastercard. This sliver is the whole business.</text>
    <rect class="wf-sw wf-aq" x="20" y="152" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/>
    <text x="40" y="158" dominant-baseline="central">Acquirer markup &#8594; the merchant's bank, for processing</text>
  </g>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c1-28">When you pay <span class="num">$100</span> at a coffee shop, the merchant does not receive <span class="num">$100.</span> It receives roughly <span class="num">$97.50.</span> That <span class="num">~$2.50</span> gap is the merchant discount rate (MDR), and it gets split three ways:</p><ul><li data-b="c1-29"><strong>Interchange</strong> — The largest slice (<span class="num">~1.5</span>–<span class="num">2%</span>) goes to the issuer as interchange — compensation for taking the credit risk and giving the cardholder a card in the first place.</li><li data-b="c1-30"><strong>Scheme fee</strong> — A smaller slice (<span class="num">~0.1</span>–<span class="num">0.3%</span>) goes to Mastercard as the scheme / assessment fee — for routing, processing, and running the rules.</li><li data-b="c1-31"><strong>Acquirer markup</strong> — The remainder goes to the acquirer as its markup — for processing on the merchant’s behalf.</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-32">Interchange is set by the network but paid by the merchant and collected by the issuer. Mastercard does NOT keep interchange — it keeps only the small scheme fee. Mastercard’s revenue is essentially a tiny toll on enormous volume: assessment fees plus service fees on billions of transactions.</div></aside><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-33">If you’ve looked at pharmacy economics, this is the spread taken by intermediaries in the drug-payment chain — the PBM, the wholesaler, the pharmacy each take a defined cut of the transaction, and the patient / plan pays the total. Interchange is the issuer’s cut for bearing risk, the same way a PBM’s margin compensates it for its role.</div></aside><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-34"><em>Anything you build that reduces fraud, lifts approval rates, or cuts chargebacks also lowers the merchant’s effective cost — which makes Mastercard’s network more attractive than Visa’s. That’s the business case template for almost every product on the network.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c1-35"><span class="num">1.4 </span> Card Mechanics — The Physical Layer</h3><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Term</th><th class="no-ann">What it is</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c1-36">PAN</td><td data-b="c1-37">Primary Account Number — the 16-digit number on the card.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-38">BIN / IIN</td><td data-b="c1-39">The first <span class="num">6</span>–<span class="num">8</span> digits of the PAN. Identifies which bank issued the card, so the network knows where to route.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-40">CVV / CVC</td><td data-b="c1-41">The <span class="num">3</span>–<span class="num">4</span> digit security code. Proves you physically hold the card (matters most online).</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-42">EMV chip</td><td data-b="c1-43">The chip standard that replaced the magnetic stripe. Generates a one-time cryptogram per transaction, which is why chip-present fraud is far harder than magstripe fraud.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-44">Contactless / NFC</td><td data-b="c1-45">Tap-to-pay. Same chip security, no insertion needed.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c1-46">Debit vs credit vs prepaid</td><td data-b="c1-47">Debit pulls from a bank balance immediately; credit draws on a line of credit the issuer extends; prepaid draws down a balance you loaded in advance.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><h3 class="sec" data-b="c1-48"><span class="num">1.5 </span> Gateway vs Processor vs Network — Don’t Mix These Up</h3><p data-b="c1-49">People from outside payments constantly confuse these three layers. They are not the same thing:</p><ul><li data-b="c1-50"><strong>Gateway / PSP</strong> — A payment gateway / PSP (Stripe, Braintree, Razorpay, Adyen) is the software layer that sits on a merchant’s checkout page. It captures the card details and hands them off. It is not the network.</li><li data-b="c1-51"><strong>Processor</strong> — A processor does the technical work of moving the transaction between parties on behalf of a bank (sometimes the same company as the acquirer).</li><li data-b="c1-52"><strong>Network</strong> — The network (Mastercard, Visa) is the rails underneath all of it — the switch the gateway ultimately routes onto, one layer deeper than any PSP.</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-53">Stripe is not a competitor to Mastercard in the way people think. Stripe is a gateway/PSP that still routes transactions over networks like Mastercard’s. Mastercard is the road; Stripe is a company that helps you get onto the road.</div></aside><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-54">Gateway vs network is like the difference between the EHR/e-prescribing software the doctor clicks in (the interface layer) versus the underlying claims switch that actually routes the transaction (the rails). The doctor experiences the software; the money and the routing happen a layer below.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c1-55"><span class="num">1.6 </span> Idempotency — The Concept That Prevents Double Charges</h3><p data-b="c1-56">Idempotency means: if the same request is sent twice, the result is the same as sending it once. It sounds like an engineering detail. In payments it is a product requirement, and it is the kind of thing people assume you already know.</p><p data-b="c1-57">Why it matters: you tap your card, the terminal sends the charge, and the network hiccups so the terminal never gets a response. It retries. Without idempotency, you get charged twice. With it, the system recognises ‘I already processed this exact transaction’ and returns the same result without charging again.</p><p data-b="c1-58">How it works: the request carries a unique idempotency key. If the server sees that key again, it returns the cached result rather than processing a second time.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-59">You have the same problem on MCE, just with different stakes. If the orchestrator retries an agent run for a specific HCP after a timeout, the system should recognise it already generated that brief and not regenerate from scratch producing a different output. Same input, same output. Identical principle — duplicate charges here, inconsistent AI output there.</div></aside><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-60">Duplicate charges, timeouts, partial refunds, and failed webhooks are where payments product work actually lives. The happy path is trivial; the edge cases are the job. Expect your early estimates to be badly wrong in that direction.</div></aside><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c1-61">No notes, <span class="num">90</span> seconds: walk the full journey of tapping your card for a <span class="num">$5</span> coffee — name all four parties plus the network, and go through authorization, clearing, and settlement, saying who pays whom and roughly when. Then answer: of the <span class="num">$5,</span> who takes what, and how much does Mastercard actually keep? Finally, explain idempotency and why a payments system breaks without it.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="1">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-1"></div>
    <div id="yn-1"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch2" data-idx="2">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 2</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="2"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Fraud, Risk &amp; Payment Performance (Your Team’s World)</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c2-0">Your manager Dhruba comes from the fraud / risk side, and your JD points at ‘Payment Performance Intelligence.’ Treat this part as the most important in the document. The goal is to hold a real conversation about fraud economics, not just define terms.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-1"><span class="num">2.1 </span> The Main Types of Fraud</h3><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Fraud type</th><th class="no-ann">What it is</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c2-2">Card-not-present (CNP)</td><td data-b="c2-3">Fraud on online / phone transactions where no physical card is presented. The biggest and fastest-growing category, because the chip’s protection doesn’t apply.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c2-4">Account takeover (ATO)</td><td data-b="c2-5">The fraudster gets into a real, legitimate account rather than using stolen card data — then transacts as if they were the genuine customer.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c2-6">Synthetic identity</td><td data-b="c2-7">A fabricated identity stitched together from real and fake data, used to build up credit history and then ‘bust out’ — max everything and vanish.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c2-8">First-party / friendly fraud</td><td data-b="c2-9">A genuine cardholder disputes a charge they actually made — sometimes dishonestly, sometimes from confusion.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-10"><span class="num">2.2 </span> Chargebacks</h3><p data-b="c2-11">A chargeback is a forced reversal of a transaction, initiated by the cardholder’s bank (the issuer) after a dispute. It costs the merchant the sale, often the goods too, plus a processing fee (typically <span class="num">$25</span>–<span class="num">$50</span>). That is why merchants care about fraud almost as much as issuers do — fraud isn’t just the issuer’s problem, it hits the merchant’s bottom line directly.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-12">A chargeback is the payments version of a retroactive claim denial or clawback. The payer already reimbursed the pharmacy, then later reverses it after an audit or dispute. Money that looked settled gets pulled back. Same sting, same operational headache.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-13"><span class="num">2.3 </span> The Core Trade-off: Precision vs Recall</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 300" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Precision and recall quadrant showing the four outcomes of a fraud decision">
  <text class="q-ax" x="200" y="18" text-anchor="middle">actually fraud</text>
  <text class="q-ax" x="480" y="18" text-anchor="middle">actually legitimate</text>
  <text class="q-ax q-rot" x="-95" y="16" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90)">we flagged it</text>
  <text class="q-ax q-rot" x="-235" y="16" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90)">we let it through</text>
  <g class="q-cell q-good"><rect x="64" y="30" width="272" height="120" rx="6"/>
    <text class="q-h" x="84" y="58">Caught it</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="84" y="82">The system worked. Fraud stopped</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="84" y="102">before any money moved.</text></g>
  <g class="q-cell q-bad"><rect x="344" y="30" width="272" height="120" rx="6"/>
    <text class="q-h" x="364" y="58">False positive</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="364" y="82">A real customer, declined. Lost sale,</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="364" y="102">lost goodwill. The expensive one.</text></g>
  <g class="q-cell q-bad"><rect x="64" y="160" width="272" height="120" rx="6"/>
    <text class="q-h" x="84" y="188">False negative</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="84" y="212">Fraud got through. A direct,</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="84" y="232">visible loss.</text></g>
  <g class="q-cell q-good"><rect x="344" y="160" width="272" height="120" rx="6"/>
    <text class="q-h" x="364" y="188">Clean approval</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="364" y="212">The overwhelming majority of</text>
    <text class="q-b" x="364" y="232">all traffic.</text></g>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c2-14">This is the single most important concept in fraud, and you will hear it constantly. It is exactly the metric pair you already know from evaluating models.</p><ul><li data-b="c2-15"><strong>Recall</strong> — Recall = of all the fraud that actually happened, what fraction did we catch? High recall = we miss very little fraud.</li><li data-b="c2-16"><strong>Precision</strong> — Precision = of everything we flagged as fraud, what fraction was actually fraud? High precision = few false alarms.</li></ul><p data-b="c2-17">The two fight each other. Turn the system stricter and you catch more fraud (recall up) but you also wrongly block more genuine customers (precision down). Loosen it and genuine customers sail through (precision up) but more fraud slips past (recall down). There is no setting that maxes both — you choose a balance based on what each error costs.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-18">You already know this pair under different names. In diagnostic testing it’s sensitivity (recall — catching all the true disease) and the flip side of specificity / false-positive rate (precision — not alarming healthy people). A fraud false-positive is a healthy patient told they might have cancer: you’ve alarmed and inconvenienced someone who was fine. A fraud false-negative is a missed diagnosis: the real threat walked through undetected. And your MCE Evaluator does the same job — scoring output quality and setting a threshold is exactly this balancing act.</div></aside><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-19">‘We declined more transactions this month’ is not automatically good. It might mean you caught more fraud — or it might mean you wrongly blocked thousands of paying customers. Every <span class="num">1%</span> shift has a real dollar cost on both sides.</div></aside><h4 class="sub" data-b="c2-20">How to talk about it to executives</h4><p data-b="c2-21">Never say ‘precision is <span class="num">80%</span>.’ Translate it into money: ‘We incorrectly decline <span class="num">200,000</span> legitimate transactions a month, which costs roughly <span class="num">$3M</span> in lost revenue and cardholder goodwill.’ That framing — errors expressed as dollars and declined customers, not percentages — is what gets leadership to act.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-22"><span class="num">2.4 </span> Risk-Based, Tiered Friction</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 250" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Friction rises in step with risk: frictionless, light, step-up, decline">
  
  <g class="fr f1">
    <rect x="20" y="16" width="640" height="46" rx="6"/>
    <text class="fr-s" x="40" y="33" dominant-baseline="central">Known card, known device, small amount</text>
    <text class="fr-a" x="40" y="51" dominant-baseline="central">Tap and go. No friction at all.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="fr f2">
    <rect x="44" y="74" width="616" height="46" rx="6"/>
    <text class="fr-s" x="64" y="91" dominant-baseline="central">New merchant, larger amount</text>
    <text class="fr-a" x="64" y="109" dominant-baseline="central">A quick 'was this you?' notification.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="fr f3">
    <rect x="68" y="132" width="592" height="46" rx="6"/>
    <text class="fr-s" x="88" y="149" dominant-baseline="central">Unusual location, new device</text>
    <text class="fr-a" x="88" y="167" dominant-baseline="central">Full step-up: OTP or biometric.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="fr f4">
    <rect x="92" y="190" width="568" height="46" rx="6"/>
    <text class="fr-s" x="112" y="207" dominant-baseline="central">Matches known fraud patterns</text>
    <text class="fr-a" x="112" y="225" dominant-baseline="central">Decline, or hold for manual review.</text>
  </g>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c2-23">The elegant answer to the precision/recall problem is: don’t apply the same check to everyone. Match the amount of friction to the risk of the transaction.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Tier</th><th class="no-ann">Signal</th><th class="no-ann">What happens</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c2-24">High trust</td><td data-b="c2-25">Known cardholder, known device, known merchant, small amount</td><td data-b="c2-26">Frictionless — tap and go, no extra step</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c2-27">Medium trust</td><td data-b="c2-28">New merchant, larger amount, slightly unusual pattern</td><td data-b="c2-29">Light friction — a quick ‘was this you?’ push notification</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c2-30">Low trust</td><td data-b="c2-31">Unusual location, very large amount, new device</td><td data-b="c2-32">Full step-up — OTP, biometric, or 3DS challenge</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c2-33">Suspicious</td><td data-b="c2-34">Matches known fraud patterns, velocity anomaly</td><td data-b="c2-35">Auto-decline or hold for manual review</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-36">This is clinical triage. In an ER you don’t give every patient a full workup — you fast-track the low-acuity cases and reserve the intensive, slow, expensive attention for the high-acuity ones. Friction proportional to risk is triage proportional to acuity. A regular at the same coffee shop every morning should never see a verification prompt; a <span class="num">$5,000</span> purchase from a new foreign merchant should.</div></aside><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-37"><em>The product should be invisible when it’s working (no friction for good customers) and visible only when needed (fast, clear resolution when fraud is real). The goal is never zero fraud — that would require declining far too many genuine customers. The goal is optimal fraud prevention at an acceptable false-positive rate.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-38"><span class="num">2.5 </span> Designing a Fraud-Detection System (Data → Model → Thresholds → Deploy → Monitor)</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 300" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Fraud system architecture: ingestion, a two-stage scoring layer, then a decision layer">
  <defs><marker id="ad" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
    <path d="M2 1L8 5L2 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--slate)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></marker></defs>
  <g class="ly"><rect x="20" y="16" width="660" height="52" rx="7"/>
    <text class="ly-h" x="40" y="36">Ingestion</text>
    <text class="ly-b" x="40" y="55">Every tap, swipe and online checkout, in real time</text></g>
  <line class="ph2" x1="350" y1="68" x2="350" y2="90" marker-end="url(#ad)"/>
  <g class="ly ly-mid"><rect x="20" y="90" width="660" height="112" rx="7"/>
    <text class="ly-h" x="40" y="110">Scoring &#8212; two stages, because depth costs money</text>
    <g class="st st-a"><rect x="44" y="124" width="290" height="60" rx="6"/>
      <text class="st-h" x="60" y="144">Stage 1 &#183; fast pass</text>
      <text class="st-b" x="60" y="164">Cheap rules on all traffic</text></g>
    <g class="st st-b"><rect x="366" y="124" width="290" height="60" rx="6"/>
      <text class="st-h" x="382" y="144">Stage 2 &#183; deep model</text>
      <text class="st-b" x="382" y="164">Only what looks suspicious</text></g>
    <line class="ph2" x1="334" y1="154" x2="366" y2="154" marker-end="url(#ad)"/></g>
  <line class="ph2" x1="350" y1="202" x2="350" y2="224" marker-end="url(#ad)"/>
  <g class="ly"><rect x="20" y="224" width="660" height="58" rx="7"/>
    <text class="ly-h" x="40" y="244">Decision</text>
    <text class="ly-b" x="40" y="264">Approve &#183; step up &#183; decline &#8212; against thresholds you set as PM</text></g>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c2-39">If anyone ever asks you to sketch how a fraud system works end to end, this is the spine. It also just helps you understand what your team’s product actually does.</p><ul><li data-b="c2-40"><strong>Data / signals</strong> — Transaction attributes (amount, merchant category, location, time, channel), cardholder behaviour history (usual spend, usual merchants, geographic range, device fingerprints), and network-level signals (is this merchant seeing weird patterns across everyone, not just this card?).</li><li data-b="c2-41"><strong>Model</strong> — A scoring engine that turns those signals into a risk score — say <span class="num">0</span> to <span class="num">1000.</span> It’s usually an ensemble: rules for known patterns (velocity checks, blocklists), ML for anomaly detection, and increasingly a transformer-style model like DI Pro for broad context.</li><li data-b="c2-42"><strong>Thresholds</strong> — This is the product decision, not an engineering one. You define the zones: below <span class="num">300</span> auto-approve, <span class="num">300</span>–<span class="num">700</span> step-up verification, above <span class="num">700</span> decline or manual review. Where you draw those lines depends on the cost of a false decline versus a missed fraud, and on the customer segment.</li><li data-b="c2-43"><strong>Deployment</strong> — Run the new model in shadow mode first — alongside the live system, scoring but not acting — until it matches or beats the current system on a holdout set. Then ramp traffic: <span class="num">5%</span>, <span class="num">25%</span>, <span class="num">100%</span>.</li><li data-b="c2-44"><strong>Monitoring</strong> — Watch for model drift (fraud patterns change, so performance decays), track the false-positive rate, and feed every disputed decline back into training. Fraud is adversarial — the model is never ‘done.’</li></ul><h4 class="sub" data-b="c2-45">The three layers, and the cost trade-off nobody mentions</h4><p data-b="c2-46">Architecturally the system is three layers: an ingestion layer receiving transaction data in real time (every tap, swipe, online checkout); a scoring layer running rules, ML anomaly detection, and potentially a transformer model like DI Pro; and a decision layer applying the thresholds you set — auto-approve, step-up, or decline.</p><p data-b="c2-47">Three trade-offs sit on top of that. Latency vs accuracy: a more thorough model catches more fraud but takes longer, and at Mastercard’s scale the budget is well under <span class="num">50ms</span>. False positives vs false negatives: the precision/recall dial from <span class="num">2.3.</span> And cost vs coverage — the one people forget. Running every transaction through the full model stack is expensive, so a common design is a two-stage filter: a fast, lightweight first pass, with only suspicious transactions sent through the deep model.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-48">The two-stage filter is the same architectural instinct you already applied on MCE. You chose batch generation for pre-call briefs — thoroughness wins, latency is irrelevant to the user. For SmartAssist, which runs live, you use configurable reasoning — lower depth for speed, higher depth for accuracy. Same principle: match the architecture to the latency and quality requirement of each use case, rather than picking one setting for everything.</div></aside><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-49">Your MCE experience maps cleanly. The thresholds decision is your quality-threshold work on the Evaluator — you, the PM, decide what score is good enough to ship. Shadow-mode deployment is the same instinct as validating agent output offline before trusting it in production. And monitoring for drift is the online-monitoring gap you’ve been closing on MCE. You’ve done this shape of work already, just with pharma intelligence instead of transactions.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-50"><span class="num">2.6 </span> The ROI of a Fraud Control (the number people forget)</h3><p data-b="c2-51">A fraud control is worth building when the fraud it prevents is worth more than the total cost of running it — and that total cost must include false positives.</p><p data-b="c2-52"><strong>ROI =</strong> (fraud prevented + chargeback costs avoided) − (build cost + operating cost + <strong>false-positive cost</strong>)</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-53">The classic mistake is ignoring false-positive cost. A control that catches <span class="num">$1M</span> of fraud but wrongly declines <span class="num">$2M</span> of legitimate transactions has a NEGATIVE ROI — even though its fraud-catch rate looks impressive. Declined good customers are a real, quantifiable loss, not a footnote.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-54"><span class="num">2.7 </span> PCI-DSS — The Compliance Frame You Need to Recognise</h3><p data-b="c2-55">PCI-DSS is the security standard that anyone storing, processing, or transmitting card data must follow. You don’t need the <span class="num">12</span> requirements memorised. You need to recognise when a product decision has a PCI implication. The core ideas: never store the CVV after authorization, encrypt cardholder data, restrict access on a need-to-know basis, and log everything.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-56">PCI-DSS is to card data what HIPAA is to PHI. Same philosophy you already live by at ZS: collect only what you need, encrypt it, limit who can see it, and keep an audit trail. If you understood why MCE could only surface MLR-approved content, you already understand ‘compliance as a hard product constraint built into the architecture, not a checkbox bolted on at launch.’</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-57"><span class="num">2.8 </span> Moving Authorization Rates — The Other Half of Your Team’s Name</h3><p data-b="c2-58">Fraud gets the attention, but ‘payment performance’ means authorization rates. These are the levers that lift approvals without loosening fraud control — know them, because this is likely the language of your roadmap.</p><ul><li data-b="c2-59"><strong>Tokenization</strong> — Dynamic tokens are trusted more by issuers because they’re bound to a device and merchant, so tokenized transactions approve at higher rates.</li><li data-b="c2-60"><strong>Smart retries</strong> — A soft decline (momentary insufficient funds, timeout, issuer system unavailable) isn’t a final no. Retrying with optimised routing or timing converts many of them.</li><li data-b="c2-61"><strong>Data enrichment</strong> — Share network-level signals with issuers so they decline less defensively. If network data shows a transaction is consistent with the cardholder’s cross-network behaviour, that signal helps the issuer approve with confidence.</li><li data-b="c2-62"><strong>3DS optimization</strong> — Apply step-up verification only where the risk profile warrants it, rather than universally. Less friction on low-risk transactions, no loosening on high-risk ones.</li></ul><h4 class="sub" data-b="c2-63">And the processing-cost levers</h4><ul><li data-b="c2-64">Batching efficiency (how transactions are grouped for clearing), network routing optimization (shortest path through the infrastructure), and reducing manual review volume (better automated decisioning means fewer transactions need a human).</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-65">The arithmetic that justifies all of it: at roughly <span class="num">159 billion</span> transactions a year, a <span class="num">1%</span> improvement in authorization rate is about <span class="num">1.59 billion</span> additional approved transactions. This is why basis points are a serious conversation here and not a rounding error.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-66"><span class="num">2.9 </span> Cross-Border Declines and Local Rails</h3><p data-b="c2-67">Two related realities that shape product design, and that a cards-only mental model completely misses.</p><ul><li data-b="c2-68"><strong>Cross-border declines</strong> — A cardholder travelling abroad gets declined more often because the issuer lacks local context — it sees an unusual country and declines defensively. A product that shares network-level travel signals (‘this cardholder has a flight booked to this country’) with the issuer can cut those declines significantly.</li><li data-b="c2-69"><strong>Local payment methods</strong> — India is the obvious example — UPI processes billions of transactions monthly at near-zero cost. A product supporting only card rails misses an enormous share of the market. For agentic infrastructure this means the agent layer should be rail-agnostic: it should work whether the transaction settles over cards, UPI, RTP/FedNow, or another local method.</li></ul><p data-b="c2-70">Agent Pay for Machines partly acknowledges this already, with multi-rail settlement across cards, accounts, and stablecoins — a product decision that concedes the world isn’t cards-only.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-71">You have made the swappable-source-system argument before. MCE’s L2 layer was built so different clients’ publication databases could plug into the same agent layer without rebuilding agents per client. Rail-agnostic agent infrastructure is the identical design principle, with payment rails in place of client data sources.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-72"><span class="num">2.10 </span> Measuring a Fraud Feature</h3><p data-b="c2-73">If asked how you’d judge whether a new fraud capability worked, the answer is two metrics that pull in opposite directions plus one that gets forgotten:</p><ul><li data-b="c2-74"><strong>Fraud catch rate</strong> — How many actual fraud cases did it correctly identify?</li><li data-b="c2-75"><strong>False positive rate</strong> — How many legitimate transactions did it wrongly flag?</li><li data-b="c2-76"><strong>Time-to-decision</strong> — How quickly can the operations team act on an alert — and did this feature reduce that time? Easy to overlook, but it’s where operational cost lives.</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-77">The framing worth memorising: a feature catching <span class="num">99%</span> of fraud but blocking <span class="num">10%</span> of legitimate transactions is worse than one catching <span class="num">90%</span> of fraud and blocking <span class="num">0.5%</span>. The business cost of a false positive — lost revenue, angry customer — often exceeds the cost of an individual missed fraud case.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c2-78"><span class="num">2.11 </span> Scenario — When the Chargeback Rate Spikes</h3><p data-b="c2-79">A worked example of how these ideas combine under pressure. Say a new promotion pushes the chargeback rate up.</p><ul><li data-b="c2-80"><strong>Days <span class="num">1</span>–<span class="num">30</span></strong> — Diagnose before acting. Pull chargeback data by merchant category, transaction size, geography, and cardholder segment. The critical question: is this real fraud exploiting the promo, or friendly fraud (genuine cardholders disputing legitimate purchases to get a refund)? The response is completely different. Real fraud → tighten scoring for transactions matching the promo pattern and add velocity checks. Friendly fraud → work with the issuer to improve dispute evidence quality so merchants can win disputes.</li><li data-b="c2-81"><strong>Days <span class="num">30</span>–<span class="num">60</span></strong> — Fix. If the promo structure itself enables the fraud, redesign the promo. If the model wasn’t calibrated for promo-driven behaviour, retrain with promo-period data. Stand up monitoring specific to promo transactions so the rate is visible in real time.</li><li data-b="c2-82"><strong>Days <span class="num">60</span>–<span class="num">90</span></strong> — Prevent recurrence. Build promo-specific fraud rules into the model before future promos launch, and add fraud risk assessment as a mandatory step in a promo launch checklist.</li></ul><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-83"><em>Notice the shape: diagnose the mechanism before choosing the intervention. Two problems with identical symptoms — a rising chargeback rate — need opposite responses. Jumping straight to ‘tighten the model’ would actively make friendly fraud worse by declining good customers.</em></div></aside><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c2-84">Explain to a non-technical friend in one minute why ‘we blocked more transactions this month’ might be bad news for a fraud team — using the precision/recall trade-off, but WITHOUT using the words ‘precision’ or ‘recall.’ Then: name the cost everyone forgets when calculating a fraud control’s ROI, list the four levers that lift authorization rates, and say why a chargeback spike needs diagnosis before intervention.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="2">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-2"></div>
    <div id="yn-2"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch3" data-idx="3">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 3</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="3"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Mastercard the Company, DI Pro, and Your Likely Team</h2>
  </header>
  <h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-0"><span class="num">3.1 </span> The Business Model, Restated</h3><p data-b="c3-1">Mastercard is a technology company that operates a payment network. It does not issue cards, hold deposits, extend credit, or decide who gets approved — issuers do all of that. Its revenue comes from assessment fees on the volume crossing its rails, transaction-processing fees, and — increasingly — value-added services like fraud detection, data analytics, and consulting. That last bucket is where your role lives.</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c3-2">At Mastercard’s scale, tiny percentages are gigantic. The network handles on the order of <span class="num">159 billion</span> transactions a year. A <span class="num">1%</span> improvement in authorization rate is over a billion additional approved transactions annually. This is why latency and accuracy obsess this company — small numbers times enormous volume.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-3"><span class="num">3.2 </span> Business Segments (Roughly)</h3><ul><li data-b="c3-4"><strong>Payments</strong> — Core consumer and commercial card products.</li><li data-b="c3-5"><strong>Cyber &amp; Intelligence</strong> — The home of Decision Intelligence, DI Pro, and Brighterion (the AI fraud-scoring company Mastercard acquired in <span class="num">2017</span>). If your role sits near fraud / risk analytics, this is the most likely organisational home.</li><li data-b="c3-6"><strong>New Payment Platforms</strong> — Real-time payments, open banking, and the newer rails.</li><li data-b="c3-7"><strong>Services</strong> — Data analytics, loyalty, consulting — the value-added services layer on top of the network.</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-8"><span class="num">3.3 </span> Decision Intelligence (DI) and DI Pro — Know These Cold</h3><p data-b="c3-9">This is the flagship fraud product family and, given Dhruba’s background, very likely adjacent to your work.</p><ul><li data-b="c3-10"><strong>Decision Intelligence (DI)</strong> — Mastercard’s real-time transaction-risk-scoring engine, in production since <span class="num">2016.</span> It scores roughly <span class="num">143 billion</span> transactions a year and returns a risk score in under <span class="num">50</span> milliseconds, so issuers can approve genuine transactions and block fraud in real time. It can score transactions on any network, not just Mastercard’s.</li><li data-b="c3-11"><strong>DI Pro</strong> — The generative-AI-enhanced upgrade. Instead of judging a transaction in isolation, it assesses the relationships between the many entities around a transaction (accounts, merchants, devices), scanning up to a trillion data points, still under <span class="num">~50ms</span>. Mastercard reports fraud-detection improvements from <span class="num">20%</span> up to <span class="num">300%</span> in initial modelling. It uses transformer-based / recurrent-neural-network techniques built largely in-house, and it won a <span class="num">2026</span> BankingTech award.</li></ul><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c3-12">DI Pro reasoning over the web of relationships around a transaction is conceptually close to what MCE does when it reasons over the relationships between an HCP, their publications, trials, and engagement history — rather than looking at one data point alone. ‘Context from the graph of related entities beats looking at the item in isolation’ is a principle you’ve already shipped.</div></aside><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c3-13"><em>At a trillion data points in <span class="num">50ms</span>, product decisions become engineering constraints. Every feature you propose has to fit the latency budget. A brilliant new signal that adds <span class="num">20ms</span> may simply be a non-starter, or it has to be pre-computed and cached. On MCE you made the mirror-image choice — running agents in overnight batch precisely because the quality you wanted couldn’t be hit at real-time latency. Same trade-off, opposite direction.</em></div></aside><h4 class="sub" data-b="c3-14">Why DI Pro augments rather than replaces — and why that matters</h4><p data-b="c3-15">Mastercard layered generative AI on top of Decision Intelligence rather than rebuilding it. That was not timidity; it is the correct pattern for a mission-critical system. DI already scores and approves <span class="num">143 billion</span> transactions a year — a system carrying years of accumulated trust. Replacing it wholesale would introduce risk no stakeholder would accept.</p><p data-b="c3-16">The augmentation approach keeps the existing system as the foundation, layers new capability on top, and lets the new layer prove itself on live traffic before more weight shifts to it. The general principle: earn the right to replace by proving value alongside the incumbent, and make the shift data-driven rather than calendar-driven.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c3-17">You did this at smaller scale on MCE. The agents don’t replace existing data sources and pipelines — they pull from existing source systems at L2 and add a synthesis layer at L3 on top. The pipeline stays the foundation; the agents add capability without breaking what already works. When you argue for augmentation over rip-and-replace at Mastercard, you’re describing something you’ve already shipped.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-18"><span class="num">3.4 </span> Tokenization and MDES</h3><p data-b="c3-19">Tokenization replaces the real card number (PAN) with a dynamic, scoped substitute — a token — that is cryptographically bound to a specific device and merchant. The real number never touches the merchant. MDES (Mastercard Digital Enablement Service) is Mastercard’s tokenization platform, and it’s what powers Apple Pay and Google Pay under the hood. Tokenized transactions get approved more readily because issuers trust them more — the token is far harder to intercept and reuse.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c3-20">A token standing in for the real card number is de-identification standing in for a real patient identifier: the sensitive original (SSN / MRN, or PAN) stays locked away and a scoped stand-in travels instead. It’s also exactly your MCE mandatory-HCP filter — a scoped credential enforced at the infrastructure level that limits what the agent can touch.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-21"><span class="num">3.5 </span> The Competitive Landscape</h3><ul><li data-b="c3-22"><strong>Visa</strong> — The direct network competitor — same four-party open-loop model.</li><li data-b="c3-23"><strong>Amex &amp; Discover</strong> — Closed-loop networks: they are BOTH the network AND the issuer, so they see the whole transaction on their own rails. Different model from Mastercard’s open network of thousands of banks.</li><li data-b="c3-24"><strong>PayPal / Stripe</strong> — Gateways / PSPs, not direct network competitors — they still ultimately route over networks like Mastercard’s.</li><li data-b="c3-25"><strong>FedNow &amp; RTP</strong> — Real-time bank-to-bank rails that can bypass card networks entirely for some use cases. Worth understanding as a structural shift, not just trivia — they are both a threat and an opportunity Mastercard is actively responding to.</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-26"><span class="num">3.6 </span> Strategy and Culture in Two Lines</h3><ul><li data-b="c3-27"><strong>Strategic themes</strong> — Mastercard’s stated strategy is often summarised as ‘expand in payments, extend services, embrace new networks.’ Agentic AI maps naturally onto ‘extend services’ (AI intelligence for issuers and merchants) and ‘embrace new networks’ (agent-to-agent commerce).</li><li data-b="c3-28"><strong>Decency Quotient</strong> — Mastercard talks about the ‘Decency Quotient’ (DQ) — the idea that how you treat people matters as much as how smart you are. In practice: direct but respectful, hold your position when you have evidence, say ‘I don’t know’ when you don’t, treat everyone’s input as mattering regardless of title.</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c3-29"><span class="num">3.7 </span> Your Team — Honestly</h3><p data-b="c3-30">Your JD points at ‘Payment Performance Intelligence’ under Dhruba. Nobody outside the team can tell you precisely what that maps to organisationally. Treat DI / DI Pro / fraud-risk analytics as your working hypothesis, not a confirmed fact, and confirm it directly and early rather than assuming.</p><h4 class="sub" data-b="c3-31">Questions worth asking in week one (that show you did the reading)</h4><ul><li data-b="c3-32">What does ‘Payment Performance Intelligence’ actually cover here — current scope and what’s on the roadmap?</li><li data-b="c3-33">Where does DI Pro adoption stand across the issuer base today, and what’s the biggest blocker to wider rollout?</li><li data-b="c3-34">What latency budget will I be designing within, and has it ever been the reason a feature got cut?</li><li data-b="c3-35">What’s the biggest unsolved problem on this team — the thing you wish you had more time or headcount for?</li></ul><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c3-36">In your own words: how does Mastercard make money, and why does a <span class="num">1%</span> change in authorization rate matter so much? Then explain the difference between DI and DI Pro in two sentences, and say which business segment you think your team most likely sits in and why.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="3">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-3"></div>
    <div id="yn-3"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch4" data-idx="4">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 4</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="4"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Agentic AI in Payments (Why They Hired You)</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c4-0">Your title is Agentic AI for a reason. This is the frontier where your MCE experience is a genuine differentiator, and it’s the part of the domain you can speak to with the most authority even on day one.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c4-1"><span class="num">4.1 </span> Agent Pay and Agent Pay for Machines</h3><ul><li data-b="c4-2"><strong>Agent Pay</strong> — Launched April <span class="num">2025</span> with partners including Microsoft and IBM. It lets AI agents make purchases on a user’s behalf, using agentic tokens — scoped credentials that define what the agent may buy, how much it may spend, and where. The Agent Pay Acceptance Framework requires an agent to be registered and verified before any agent-initiated transaction clears the network.</li><li data-b="c4-3"><strong>Agent Pay for Machines (AP4M)</strong> — Launched June <span class="num">2026.</span> Extends the idea to machine-to-machine commerce and microtransactions, supporting multi-rail settlement across cards, accounts, and stablecoins, with <span class="num">30</span>+ partners including Stripe, Coinbase, and Adyen.</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c4-4">The strategic pattern to remember: Mastercard partnered on distribution (the checkout and agent providers) but kept the trust infrastructure — agent registration, verification, credential management — in-house. It’s positioning itself as the trust and governance layer for autonomous commerce: not just processing transactions, but verifying who — or what — is making them.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c4-5"><span class="num">4.2 </span> How Agentic Transactions Break the Old Fraud Model</h3><ul><li data-b="c4-6"><strong>Identity</strong> — Traditional fraud models assume a human is present — there’s a card-present or biometric signal. An agent transaction has neither. You need a new identity layer answering: is this agent registered, verified, and authorized to act for this cardholder?</li><li data-b="c4-7"><strong>Behavioural baselines</strong> — Fraud models are trained on human rhythms — spending velocity, geography, time of day. A legitimate agent might make <span class="num">50</span> purchases in <span class="num">5</span> minutes across <span class="num">10</span> merchants — behaviour that would instantly flag any human. Agent transactions need their own separate baselines.</li><li data-b="c4-8"><strong>Accountability chain</strong> — If an agent is compromised (say by prompt injection) and overspends, you must be able to trace: was the credential valid, were the spending limits enforced, did the agent exceed its scoped permissions, and therefore who is liable?</li></ul><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c4-9">Agentic tokens are your MCE SQL-layer guardrails, one-for-one. A scoped credential enforced at the infrastructure level — not a polite instruction in a prompt, but a hard limit the system cannot exceed. Your mandatory HCP filter (the agent can only ever touch data for authorized doctors) is conceptually identical to a spending limit on an agentic token (the agent can only ever spend within set bounds). You have shipped this exact idea; only the nouns change.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c4-10"><span class="num">4.3 </span> Where Agentic Commerce Creates Value — and Risk</h3><ul><li data-b="c4-11"><strong>Most value</strong> — B2B commerce and procurement. Routine, high-volume, rules-based purchasing — restocking supplies, paying recurring invoices, managing vendors — is exactly what agents can do autonomously, with humans reviewing exceptions. It saves hours and it grows Mastercard’s transaction volume.</li><li data-b="c4-12"><strong>Most risk</strong> — Fraud and accountability. Every link in the human authorization-intent-liability chain gets complicated when an agent acts. The bet Mastercard is making is that whoever gets governance right first wins the market — because agentic commerce is going to happen faster than governance frameworks can keep up.</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c4-13"><span class="num">4.4 </span> Who Bears Liability — and Why It Decides Your Product Design</h3><p data-b="c4-14">This is the most underrated idea in agentic payments, and it is squarely a PM concern rather than a legal footnote.</p><p data-b="c4-15">In a standard tokenized transaction, if the token was validly issued and the merchant followed the authentication protocol, liability typically shifts to the issuer — it accepted the risk when it approved. In an agentic transaction it gets harder. If the user set up the agent, gave it spending parameters, and it acted within them, the user bears responsibility. If the agent was compromised or exceeded its permissions, liability should fall on whoever’s security was breached — possibly the platform that built the agent, the merchant that accepted the transaction, or the network that verified the credential.</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c4-16">Why this is your problem: the liability framework directly determines the permission model. If the issuer bears liability, the issuer wants tight controls on agent spending limits and transaction types. If the merchant bears liability, the merchant wants strong verification that the agent is legitimate. You design the permission model, the spending-limits UI, and the dispute workflow around who is on the hook. Product design follows the liability model, not the other way around.</div></aside><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c4-17"><em>When you’re handed an agentic feature to spec, ask ‘who pays when this goes wrong?’ before asking ‘what should the interface look like?’ The answer to the first question constrains the second. Getting this order backwards produces designs that legal and risk will reject late, which is the expensive way to learn it.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c4-18"><span class="num">4.5 </span> Your Bridge, In One Paragraph</h3><p data-b="c4-19">When someone asks why a pharma person is building agentic AI for payments, the honest and strong answer is: the domain changes but the underlying PM problem doesn’t. You design multi-agent systems that produce trustworthy, grounded output at the exact point where a human (or now another agent) has to act on it fast, with real consequences if the AI is wrong. That was true when the human was an MSL walking into a KOL meeting; it’s true when the actor is a payments agent making a real-time purchase. You’re not pretending to have payments domain expertise — you’re bringing agentic architecture and AI-output-quality ownership into a new domain, which is precisely what a team hiring its first cohort of agentic-AI PMs needs.</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c4-20">The one gap to name honestly: scale and latency. MCE runs batch, prep-ahead-of-time, where latency isn’t a hard constraint. Payments demands real-time decisions at vastly higher volume — a genuinely different engineering and product problem. Say so directly rather than claim false familiarity. Naming it earns more credibility than hiding it.</div></aside><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c4-21">Explain, without notes: what is an agentic token, and how is it the same idea as a guardrail you already built on MCE? Then give the one-paragraph answer to ‘why should a pharma PM build payments AI’ — and name the one gap you’d admit to.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="4">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-4"></div>
    <div id="yn-4"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch5" data-idx="5">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 5</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="5"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Trends, Standards &amp; Key Metrics</h2>
  </header>
  <h3 class="sec" data-b="c5-0"><span class="num">5.1 </span> Industry Trends to Recognise</h3><ul><li data-b="c5-1"><strong>Open Banking</strong> — Banks sharing customer data with third parties through APIs (with consent), enabling new financial products built on top of bank data.</li><li data-b="c5-2"><strong>Real-time payments</strong> — FedNow (US, launched July <span class="num">2023</span>) and The Clearing House’s RTP network (launched <span class="num">2017</span>). Money moves bank-to-bank in seconds, any time, instead of in next-day batches.</li><li data-b="c5-3"><strong>Buy Now Pay Later (BNPL)</strong> — Installment payments at checkout — split a purchase into scheduled chunks.</li><li data-b="c5-4"><strong>Digital wallets</strong> — Apple Pay, Google Pay — tokenized cards living on a phone, increasingly replacing the physical card.</li><li data-b="c5-5"><strong>Agentic payments</strong> — AI agents transacting on a user’s behalf — Mastercard’s Agent Pay and AP4M, covered in Part <span class="num">4.</span></li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c5-6"><span class="num">5.2 </span> ISO <span class="num">8583</span> vs ISO <span class="num">20022 </span>(the messaging standards)</h3><p data-b="c5-7">These are the languages banks and networks use to describe a transaction to each other. ISO <span class="num">8583</span> is the old standard — a fixed, compact, limited-format message. ISO <span class="num">20022</span> is the newer global standard — rich, structured, far more data per message. The whole industry is migrating from the first to the second.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c5-8">The cleanest analogy: ISO <span class="num">8583</span> is a telegram — terse, rigid, minimal. ISO <span class="num">20022</span> is an email with attachments — structured, detailed, extensible. (If you want a healthcare parallel you’ll recognise the shape of: it’s roughly the leap from old fixed-format HL7 v2 messages to richer modern structured standards — though you don’t need clinical-IT depth here, just the ‘terse old format → rich new format’ idea.)</div></aside><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c5-9"><em>Richer transaction data means better inputs for fraud models, better merchant analytics, and more context for AI agents to reason over. More data per message is a tailwind for exactly the kind of intelligence products your team builds.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c5-10"><span class="num">5.3 </span> The KPIs You’ll Hear Every Week</h3><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Metric</th><th class="no-ann">What it means and why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c5-11">Authorization rate</td><td data-b="c5-12">Share of transactions successfully approved. Even a <span class="num">1%</span> lift is enormous at network scale (<span class="num">~1.59B</span> more approved transactions/year).</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c5-13">API / transaction latency</td><td data-b="c5-14">How fast a transaction processes. Milliseconds decide whether a checkout feels instant or broken.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c5-15">Fraud rate</td><td data-b="c5-16">Fraudulent transactions as a share of volume, usually quoted in basis points.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c5-17">False-positive rate</td><td data-b="c5-18">Legitimate transactions wrongly flagged as fraud. As important as catching fraud — every false positive is a frustrated paying customer.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c5-19">Chargeback rate</td><td data-b="c5-20">Share of transactions that get reversed after dispute. A direct hit to merchants and a fraud signal.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c5-21">List the five KPIs above from memory and say, for each, why a payments team loses money if it moves the wrong way. Then explain the ISO <span class="num">8583 </span>→ <span class="num">20022</span> shift in one sentence a non-technical person would understand.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="5">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-5"></div>
    <div id="yn-5"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch6" data-idx="6">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 6</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="6"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>The Actual Problem Your Team Is Solving</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c6-0">Everything up to here is background. This part is the job. If you understand only one thing before day one, make it this.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c6-1"><span class="num">6.1 </span> The One-Sentence Version</h3><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c6-2">Mastercard spent decades training its fraud systems to treat bots as thieves. Now, with Agent Pay and agentic commerce, it has to teach those same systems to let authorized agents transact — without opening the door to the real bots. That is the seam your team sits on.</div></aside><p data-b="c6-3">Mastercard’s Chief AI and Data Officer, Greg Ulrich, said it almost exactly that way in public:</p><blockquote class="q" data-b="c6-4"><em>“We’ve built a bunch of risk rules over time that were intended to stop a bot from transacting. Now we need to enable the bot to transact, so that requires a change to our risk framework and our risk rules.”</em>   — Greg Ulrich, Chief AI &amp; Data Officer, Mastercard</blockquote><p data-b="c6-5">Read that twice. It is the product problem, the fraud problem, and the reason a role like yours exists, all in one sentence.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c6-6"><span class="num">6.2 </span> Why Agents Look Exactly Like Fraud</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 250" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Comparison of human and agent signals at checkout, showing why agents trip fraud models">
  <text class="sg-h sg-hum" x="150" y="22" text-anchor="middle">A human checking out</text>
  <text class="sg-h sg-agt" x="510" y="22" text-anchor="middle">An agent checking out</text>
  
  <g class="sg-row">
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ch" x="20" y="40" width="260" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="36" y="59" dominant-baseline="central">Hesitates, scrolls, mistypes</text>
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ca" x="380" y="40" width="300" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="396" y="59" dominant-baseline="central">No behavioural signal at all</text>
    <text class="sg-v" x="330" y="59" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">vs</text>
  </g>
  <g class="sg-row">
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ch" x="20" y="90" width="260" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="36" y="109" dominant-baseline="central">Familiar phone or laptop</text>
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ca" x="380" y="90" width="300" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="396" y="109" dominant-baseline="central">A cloud server it has never seen</text>
    <text class="sg-v" x="330" y="109" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">vs</text>
  </g>
  <g class="sg-row">
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ch" x="20" y="140" width="260" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="36" y="159" dominant-baseline="central">Browses, compares, lingers</text>
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ca" x="380" y="140" width="300" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="396" y="159" dominant-baseline="central">Straight to checkout, seconds</text>
    <text class="sg-v" x="330" y="159" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">vs</text>
  </g>
  <g class="sg-row">
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ch" x="20" y="190" width="260" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="36" y="209" dominant-baseline="central">One purchase at a time</text>
    <rect class="sg-c sg-ca" x="380" y="190" width="300" height="38" rx="5"/>
    <text class="sg-t" x="396" y="209" dominant-baseline="central">Fifty purchases in five minutes</text>
    <text class="sg-v" x="330" y="209" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">vs</text>
  </g>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c6-7">An AI agent checking out behaves, on every signal a fraud model was built to read, like an attack. This is not a philosophical problem — it is a concrete detection problem:</p><ul><li data-b="c6-8"><strong>No human signals</strong> — Agents don’t hesitate, scroll, or mistype. Behavioural biometrics — mouse movement, typing rhythm, scroll patterns — simply produce nothing. As one security vendor put it, agents are bots by design.</li><li data-b="c6-9"><strong>Wrong device fingerprint</strong> — Agents run from cloud servers, not the cardholder’s phone or laptop. Device fingerprinting, one of the strongest legacy fraud signals, breaks.</li><li data-b="c6-10"><strong>Too fast, too efficient</strong> — An agent moves through checkout far faster than a human, visits fewer pages, and explores less. Speed and efficiency were historically fraud signals.</li><li data-b="c6-11"><strong>Aggregate-level attack</strong> — A compromised agent can be prompted to ‘fix the payment issue’ and will execute a sequence of steps that individually look normal but in aggregate resemble a fraud workflow.</li></ul><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c6-12">You have seen the shape of this problem before. On MCE, the Evaluator had to distinguish a genuinely good agent output from one that merely looked well-formed — fluent, confident, and wrong. Here the system has to distinguish a legitimate agent from a malicious one when both produce identical surface signals. In both cases the old detection heuristics stop working and you have to define new ground truth. That is a PM problem as much as a modelling one.</div></aside><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c6-13">The threat is real and growing, not theoretical. Visa’s payment-risk team reported a <span class="num">25%</span> increase in malicious bot-initiated transactions (<span class="num">40%</span> in the US) and a more than <span class="num">450%</span> rise in dark-web posts mentioning ‘AI Agent’ over six months in <span class="num">2025.</span> Every legitimate agent framework has to be built assuming adversaries are already using the same techniques.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c6-14"><span class="num">6.3 </span> The Money Behind the Trade-off</h3><p data-b="c6-15">Part <span class="num">2</span> covered precision vs recall conceptually. Here are the numbers that make it a boardroom issue, and the reason ‘payment performance’ and ‘fraud’ are the same conversation at Mastercard.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Figure</th><th class="no-ann">What it means</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c6-16"><span class="num">~$118B / year</span></td><td data-b="c6-17">Estimated US merchant losses from falsely declined legitimate transactions (Javelin). Directional and dated, but widely cited.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c6-18"><span class="num">~$9B / year</span></td><td data-b="c6-19">Estimated cost of actual verified card fraud, for comparison. False declines cost roughly 13x more than the fraud itself.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c6-20"><span class="num">32%</span></td><td data-b="c6-21">Share of consumers who say they won’t shop with a merchant again after being declined. The loss compounds beyond the single sale.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><p data-b="c6-22">Mastercard’s own merchant-facing material states the link plainly: decisions made to stop fraud directly affect authorization rates, customer experience, and long-term revenue. That is why a team can be named for ‘payment performance’ and spend its days on fraud models — they are two ends of one dial.</p><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c6-23"><em>When you propose anything, be ready to express it in both currencies at once: how much fraud does this stop, and how many good customers does it stop with it? A proposal that only answers one half will not survive review.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c6-24"><span class="num">6.4 </span> The Design Principle to Internalise</h3><p data-b="c6-25">Ulrich was explicit about how Mastercard believes agentic systems must be built, and it is a direct instruction for how you write requirements:</p><blockquote class="q" data-b="c6-26"><em>“The guardrails, the security, all this stuff has to be embedded at the front end. These can’t be things that we’re adding on at the back end… If you’re trying to extend that and then add guardrails in as you’re extending it, once you’ve already built it, I think you’re doomed to fail.”</em>   — Greg Ulrich, Mastercard</blockquote><p data-b="c6-27">He also stressed that observability and accountability matter as much as the intelligence itself — being able to prove what an agent did and why, not just that it performed well.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c6-28">This is the same argument you already make about SQL-layer guardrails on MCE: a hard engineering constraint the system cannot exceed beats a polite instruction in a prompt. You have shipped this belief. Here it is stated as company philosophy by a C-level executive — which means when you argue it in a design review, you are arguing with the grain, not against it.</div></aside><p data-b="c6-29">One more nuance worth carrying in: delegation makes the transaction fundamentally harder to reason about. Ulrich’s framing was that instead of a single atomic transaction, you are delegating authority — so trust depends on identifying the intent, the behaviours, and the constraints that were meant to apply.</p><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c6-30">In one minute, no notes: explain why an AI agent completing a checkout trips a traditional fraud model, name three specific signals that break, and then explain why simply loosening the rules is the wrong answer. Finish with the Ulrich principle about where guardrails belong.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="6">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-6"></div>
    <div id="yn-6"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch7" data-idx="7">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 7</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="7"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>What You Actually Own as an Agentic AI PM</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c7-0">This is the part to be most honest with yourself about. On MCE, Chayan owned the agent-building and the scoring infrastructure. You owned product decisions on top of it. At Mastercard, in a role titled Agentic AI, more of the definition work will sit with you — and the vocabulary of that ownership is learnable before you start.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c7-1"><span class="num">7.1 </span> The Ownership Map</h3><p data-b="c7-2">Across real agentic-AI PM job descriptions and practitioner writing, these are the workstreams that belong to the PM rather than the engineer or the data scientist.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">You own</th><th class="no-ann">What that means concretely</th><th class="no-ann">You do NOT own</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c7-3">Problem &amp; scope</td><td data-b="c7-4">What the agent is for, and — more importantly — what it must never do</td><td data-b="c7-5">Model architecture</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-6">Definition of ‘good’</td><td data-b="c7-7">The quality dimensions scored (accuracy, grounding, relevance, safety, completeness) and why those</td><td data-b="c7-8">How scoring is implemented</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-9">The eval set</td><td data-b="c7-10">Golden examples, expected outputs, edge-case coverage, and what triggers a new case</td><td data-b="c7-11">The eval harness / infra</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-12">Launch thresholds</td><td data-b="c7-13">The score or pass rate required to ship, and what happens when production drops below it</td><td data-b="c7-14">Model tuning to hit it</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-15">The autonomy ladder</td><td data-b="c7-16">What the agent does alone, what needs approval, what it may never do unattended</td><td data-b="c7-17">Orchestration framework choice</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-18">Guardrail policy</td><td data-b="c7-19">Which rules are hard infrastructure constraints vs. prompt instructions</td><td data-b="c7-20">Guardrail implementation</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-21">Product-level metrics</td><td data-b="c7-22">Task completion, human-rescue rate, cost per successful task, p95 latency</td><td data-b="c7-23">Instrumentation plumbing</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c7-24">The feedback loop</td><td data-b="c7-25">How a production failure becomes a new golden example or a policy change</td><td data-b="c7-26">Retraining pipeline</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><h3 class="sec" data-b="c7-27"><span class="num">7.2 </span> The Autonomy Ladder</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 220" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="Three rungs of agent autonomy, climbed only as risk falls">
  
  <g class="rg r3">
    <rect x="140" y="16" width="440" height="52" rx="6"/>
    <text class="rg-h" x="160" y="36" dominant-baseline="central">Rung 3</text>
    <text class="rg-b" x="160" y="55" dominant-baseline="central">Acts alone inside guardrails. Humans review exceptions.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="rg r2">
    <rect x="80" y="80" width="500" height="52" rx="6"/>
    <text class="rg-h" x="100" y="100" dominant-baseline="central">Rung 2</text>
    <text class="rg-b" x="100" y="119" dominant-baseline="central">Takes steps, but a human approves before anything commits.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="rg r1">
    <rect x="20" y="144" width="560" height="52" rx="6"/>
    <text class="rg-h" x="40" y="164" dominant-baseline="central">Rung 1</text>
    <text class="rg-b" x="40" y="183" dominant-baseline="central">Only suggests. A human does everything.</text>
  </g>
  <text class="rg-ax" x="614" y="42" dominant-baseline="central">more</text>
  <text class="rg-ax" x="614" y="60" dominant-baseline="central">autonomy</text>
  <text class="rg-ax" x="614" y="180" dominant-baseline="central">more</text>
  <text class="rg-ax" x="614" y="198" dominant-baseline="central">control</text>
</svg></figure><p data-b="c7-28">The clearest mental model for agentic product design is a ladder of autonomy, and deciding which rung a capability sits on is a PM call, not an engineering one.</p><ul><li data-b="c7-29"><strong>Rung <span class="num">1</span></strong> — The agent only suggests. A human does everything. Lowest risk, lowest leverage.</li><li data-b="c7-30"><strong>Rung <span class="num">2</span></strong> — The agent takes partial steps but a human approves before anything commits.</li><li data-b="c7-31"><strong>Rung <span class="num">3</span></strong> — The agent acts independently within defined guardrails, with humans reviewing exceptions.</li></ul><p data-b="c7-32">You climb the ladder as risk drops and task clarity rises — never the reverse, and never all at once. This is exactly the logic behind Agent Pay’s consumer controls: spend caps, merchant-category limits, and step-up authentication for higher-value purchases are the rungs made concrete.</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c7-33">A useful line to remember and reuse: a chatbot can embarrass you; an agent can damage something. That difference is the entire reason guardrails and autonomy design are PM work rather than a technical afterthought.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c7-34"><span class="num">7.3 </span> Evaluation — The Capability That Separates a Real AI PM</h3><p data-b="c7-35">Of everything in this part, owning the eval set is the single thing that most distinguishes a genuine AI PM from a strong generalist PM who has read about LLMs. The principle is blunt: if you cannot clearly define what ‘good’ means, you cannot safely ship.</p><h4 class="sub" data-b="c7-36">What a real agent eval covers</h4><ul><li data-b="c7-37"><strong>Task completion rate</strong> — Did the agent fully complete the user’s goal, end to end, without a human rescuing it? This is the headline number.</li><li data-b="c7-38"><strong>Tool correctness</strong> — Did it call the right tool, with the right inputs, at the right time?</li><li data-b="c7-39"><strong>Step completion</strong> — Did it get through the intended sequence, or stall partway?</li><li data-b="c7-40"><strong>Tool error / fallback rate</strong> — How often did it fail a tool call and fall back — and how gracefully?</li><li data-b="c7-41"><strong>Looping</strong> — Did it get stuck repeating itself? A distinctly agentic failure mode that single-turn evals never catch.</li></ul><p data-b="c7-42">Evals should be continuous release gates, not a one-time offline exercise before launch. This is precisely the gap you already identified honestly on MCE — the Evaluator scoring offline but not yet wired into the production loop. Arriving at Mastercard already knowing that this gap is the norm rather than an embarrassment, and knowing what closing it looks like, is a real advantage.</p><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c7-43">Your MCE Evaluator scored grounding, relevance, and completeness into a composite. That is the same instinct as the metrics above — you have already designed a scoring rubric and argued about thresholds with a technical lead. What you have less practice in is authoring the golden set yourself and defending edge-case coverage. That is the specific thing to go learn from your ZS peers before you leave.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c7-44"><span class="num">7.4 </span> Observability — Two Audiences, Two Views</h3><p data-b="c7-45">Agent observability serves two different people and a good PM builds for both:</p><ul><li data-b="c7-46"><strong>Engineers need</strong> — Traces, failure breakdowns, individual run inspection — what broke and where.</li><li data-b="c7-47"><strong>Product &amp; leadership need</strong> — Trend views — task success rate, human handoff rate, cost per successful task. This is what decides whether the agent scales or gets shut down.</li></ul><p data-b="c7-48">Also watch token usage, tool-call volume, and p95 latency per step, with alerts on meaningful deviation. In a payments context, latency is not a nice-to-have metric — it is a hard budget.</p><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c7-49"><em>At Mastercard’s scale, product decisions become engineering constraints. Fraud scoring runs in well under <span class="num">100ms</span>; DI Pro reportedly evaluates on the order of a trillion data points and returns a score in under <span class="num">~50ms</span>. A feature that adds <span class="num">20ms</span> may simply be impossible, or must be pre-computed and cached. On MCE you made the mirror-image call — batch overnight generation precisely because your quality bar could not be met in real time. Same trade-off, opposite constraint.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c7-50"><span class="num">7.5 </span> Questions to Ask Your ZS Peers Before You Leave</h3><p data-b="c7-51">You still have notice-period access to PMs building agents. Use it. Ask to see the artefacts, not just hear descriptions — twenty minutes looking at a real eval sheet teaches more than an hour of explanation.</p><h4 class="sub" data-b="c7-52">Ask to physically see these two</h4><ul><li data-b="c7-53">Their eval spreadsheet or golden-example set. Look at the format: how a row is structured, how many examples, how the expected output is written, who authored the ‘correct’ answers, and how edge cases were chosen.</li><li data-b="c7-54">A real requirements doc or story they wrote for an agent feature. This shows you the exact line between what a PM specifies and what is left to engineering judgement — in practice, not in theory.</li></ul><h4 class="sub" data-b="c7-55">Ask these in conversation</h4><ul><li data-b="c7-56">What did you write before any prompt existed — and what did you explicitly rule out of scope?</li><li data-b="c7-57">What are the scoring dimensions, and did you define them before or after seeing the model’s first outputs? (Before is right. After means the bar was set to match reality rather than the need.)</li><li data-b="c7-58">What score or pass rate was required to ship, who set that number, and what happens in production when it drops below?</li><li data-b="c7-59">What can the agent do with no human in the loop, and who drew that line?</li><li data-b="c7-60">Which rules are hard engineering constraints and which are prompt instructions — how did you decide which category each belongs in?</li><li data-b="c7-61">What is the worst thing this agent could get wrong, and what catches it before a user sees it?</li><li data-b="c7-62">What dashboard do you personally check — yours, not the engineer’s?</li><li data-b="c7-63">Walk me through exactly how a production failure becomes a new golden example or a prompt change.</li><li data-b="c7-64">Did you ever pick a cheaper or faster model over a more accurate one? What drove that call?</li></ul><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c7-65">Without notes: list the eight things an agentic AI PM owns, and for each, name the adjacent thing you do NOT own. Then explain the autonomy ladder and give an example of a capability at each rung.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="7">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-7"></div>
    <div id="yn-7"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch8" data-idx="8">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 8</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="8"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Your First 90 Days</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c8-0">The consensus arc across PM thought leadership is three thirty-day sprints: discovery, then a quick win, then trust. The dominant failure mode is arriving with answers and importing the playbook from your last company. Coming from ZS into payments, that is the exact trap to avoid.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c8-1"><span class="num">8.1 </span> Days <span class="num">1</span>–<span class="num">30 </span>— Discovery (Listen, Don’t Import)</h3><figure class="dgw no-ann">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 150" class="dg" role="img" aria-label="First 90 days: discovery, then one quick win, then trust">
  <line class="tl-r" x1="30" y1="44" x2="660" y2="44"/>
  
  <g class="tl">
    <circle cx="30" cy="44" r="6"/>
    <text class="tl-d" x="30" y="24" text-anchor="start">Days 1&#8211;30</text>
    <text class="tl-t" x="30" y="76">Discovery</text>
    <text class="tl-b" x="30" y="98">Listen. Do not import</text>
    <text class="tl-b" x="30" y="116">the ZS playbook.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="tl">
    <circle cx="250" cy="44" r="6"/>
    <text class="tl-d" x="250" y="24" text-anchor="start">Days 31&#8211;60</text>
    <text class="tl-t" x="250" y="76">One quick win</text>
    <text class="tl-b" x="250" y="98">Small and finished beats</text>
    <text class="tl-b" x="250" y="116">large and pending.</text>
  </g>
  <g class="tl">
    <circle cx="470" cy="44" r="6"/>
    <text class="tl-d" x="470" y="24" text-anchor="start">Days 61&#8211;90</text>
    <text class="tl-t" x="470" y="76">Trust</text>
    <text class="tl-b" x="470" y="98">Argue with evidence.</text>
    <text class="tl-b" x="470" y="116">Stake one claim.</text>
  </g>
</svg></figure><ul><li data-b="c8-2"><strong>Two backlogs</strong> — Run two backlogs in parallel: a learning backlog (what you need to understand) and a relationship backlog (who you need to know). Treat the second as seriously as the first — at Mastercard specifically, progression is widely described as depending on cross-functional relationships.</li><li data-b="c8-3"><strong>Use the product</strong> — Use the products end to end yourself. Understand the current state versus the stated ambition.</li><li data-b="c8-4"><strong>Clarify expectations twice</strong> — Get role expectations from two directions: from Dhruba (vision, what success looks like at <span class="num">6</span> and <span class="num">12</span> months) and from your immediate team (what the day-to-day actually is).</li><li data-b="c8-5"><strong>Find the north star</strong> — Learn the current authorization-rate and false-decline benchmarks, and find exactly where existing risk rules break for agent traffic. That intersection is your team’s north star.</li></ul><h4 class="sub" data-b="c8-6">High-value questions for the first weeks</h4><ul><li data-b="c8-7">What does ‘Payment Performance Intelligence’ actually cover here — current scope and roadmap?</li><li data-b="c8-8">What are our authorization-rate and false-decline benchmarks today?</li><li data-b="c8-9">Where do existing DI risk rules break for agent-initiated traffic?</li><li data-b="c8-10">What is the eval and guardrail framework for our agents today, and who owns it?</li><li data-b="c8-11">Who are the issuer, acquirer, and merchant design partners, and what do they actually need?</li><li data-b="c8-12">What latency budget must I design within, and has it ever killed a feature?</li><li data-b="c8-13">Where are decision rights ambiguous? (In a heavily matrixed org, this is the most valuable question nobody asks.)</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c8-14"><span class="num">8.2 </span> Days <span class="num">31</span>–<span class="num">60 </span>— One Quick Win</h3><ul><li data-b="c8-15"><strong>Pick something small</strong> — Something contained and shippable: a documented friction log of an agent flow, a first-pass eval rubric, or a tight PRD for one narrowly scoped capability. Small and finished beats ambitious and pending.</li><li data-b="c8-16"><strong>Use their format</strong> — Mastercard product teams use Amazon-style ‘Working Backwards’ — start from the customer outcome or a mock press release and work back to requirements. Write your first artefact in the format they already use rather than the format you used at ZS.</li><li data-b="c8-17"><strong>Go to the edge cases</strong> — Learn the exceptions, not the happy path. In payments the happy path is trivial; the value is in declines, timeouts, idempotency, partial refunds, duplicate charges, failed webhooks, 3DS abandonment, and chargebacks.</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c8-18">A payments PM writing publicly about scale put the ramp shock plainly: edge cases are around <span class="num">80%</span> of payment work, and most PMs underestimate this by roughly 10x. A ‘one-day’ Stripe integration took him three weeks. Expect your early estimates to be wrong in the same direction.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c8-19"><span class="num">8.3 </span> Days <span class="num">61</span>–<span class="num">90 </span>— Trust</h3><ul><li data-b="c8-20"><strong>Be the go-to</strong> — Become the person people come to for your product area — customers, product, strategy, metrics, competitors.</li><li data-b="c8-21"><strong>Argue with evidence</strong> — Form hypotheses backed by research and data, and communicate as a partner to Dhruba and stakeholders rather than as someone still ramping.</li><li data-b="c8-22"><strong>Stake one claim</strong> — Propose — don’t yet execute — a point of view on one meaningful roadmap bet at the fraud/agentic seam.</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c8-23"><span class="num">8.4 </span> Adjust Based on What You Find</h3><ul><li data-b="c8-24"><strong>If it’s 0-to-<span class="num">1</span></strong> — If the team is pre-product / 0-to-<span class="num">1,</span> shift earlier toward prototyping and defining the eval framework yourself. Your MCE experience is most directly relevant here.</li><li data-b="c8-25"><strong>If it’s mature</strong> — If it’s a mature DI/DI Pro surface, weight toward learning existing metrics, model-risk governance, and issuer needs before proposing changes.</li><li data-b="c8-26"><strong>If there’s urgency</strong> — If Dhruba signals urgency for a specific deliverable in your first <span class="num">1</span>:<span class="num">1,</span> compress discovery and pair it with the quick win.</li></ul><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c8-27">Write your own <span class="num">30</span>/<span class="num">60</span>/<span class="num">90</span> in one page — what you will learn, who you will meet, and what single artefact you will ship by day <span class="num">60.</span> Then list the three questions you most want answered in your first week with Dhruba.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="8">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-8"></div>
    <div id="yn-8"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch9" data-idx="9">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 9</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="9"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Stakeholders and Influence</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c9-0">Payments has a broader and more trust-intensive stakeholder web than most industries, because value and risk are spread across the four-party model plus processors, gateways, and now AI platforms. No single party has authority over the others — which makes influence-without-authority the default operating mode, not an exception.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c9-1"><span class="num">9.1 </span> The Map</h3><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Group</th><th class="no-ann">Who and why they matter</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c9-2">Engineering</td><td data-b="c9-3">Build partners. Your credibility here comes from asking sharp technical questions, not from issuing directives.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-4">Data science / ML &amp; MLOps</td><td data-b="c9-5">Own model architecture, training, drift monitoring. Your counterpart relationship — the Chayan equivalent.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-6">Data engineering</td><td data-b="c9-7">The pipelines feeding the models. Data quality problems surface here first.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-8">UX / design</td><td data-b="c9-9">Agent consent flows, spend controls, step-up authentication moments — all UX-heavy.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-10">Compliance, legal &amp; risk</td><td data-b="c9-11">In payments these are product inputs from day one, not a launch gate. Engage early.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-12">Security</td><td data-b="c9-13">Agent identity, credential management, and the adversarial side of agentic commerce.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-14">Fraud / risk teams</td><td data-b="c9-15">Likely your closest business partner given the team’s position.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-16">Technical program managers</td><td data-b="c9-17">Mastercard’s PM-Technical model pairs you with TPMs; they run delivery, you run definition.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c9-18">Other PMs &amp; leadership</td><td data-b="c9-19">Roadmap alignment in a matrixed org where many teams have input and few have clear decision rights.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><h4 class="sub" data-b="c9-20">External</h4><ul><li data-b="c9-21"><strong>Issuers / banks</strong> — They consume the fraud scores and make the approve/decline call. Their trust is the product’s adoption ceiling.</li><li data-b="c9-22"><strong>Acquirers &amp; merchants</strong> — Care about authorization rates, chargebacks, and cost. Agent traffic is their new unknown.</li><li data-b="c9-23"><strong>AI platform partners</strong> — Microsoft, OpenAI, Google and the checkout/wallet ecosystem — partners in the agentic stack, not just vendors.</li><li data-b="c9-24"><strong>Regulators</strong> — A permanent stakeholder in payments, not an occasional one.</li></ul><p data-b="c9-25">Mastercard’s own PM job descriptions call explicitly for discovery with issuers, acquirers, processors, and banks — so external discovery is part of the job description, not an optional extra.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c9-26"><span class="num">9.2 </span> Influence Without Authority</h3><p data-b="c9-27">The standard framing: you are accountable for outcomes but own neither the people nor the resources. The three currencies are credibility (earned, never granted by title), reciprocity (help others hit their goals), and vision (a clear picture of where this goes).</p><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c9-28">For a domain newcomer, credibility must be earned fast. The blunt version from an engineering leader: without trust and credibility you won’t own the initiative — you’ll merely be ‘looped in.’ The fastest route to credibility in this specific role is evaluation fluency plus payments-economics fluency, which is exactly what Parts <span class="num">1</span>–<span class="num">7</span> of this document are for.</div></aside><aside class="c c-bridge"><span class="c-l no-ann">From your world</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c9-29">This is not new to you. At ZS you led a cross-functional team of <span class="num">15</span>+ with no direct reports across a large portfolio — entirely influence-based. The environment changes; the muscle is the same one you already use every day.</div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c9-30"><span class="num">9.3 </span> Your Bridge From Healthcare — Name It Explicitly</h3><p data-b="c9-31">Your pharma/ZS background is an asset in three specific, defensible ways. Say these out loud when you need to establish why you belong:</p><ul><li data-b="c9-32"><strong>Regulated-industry instinct</strong> — You already treat compliance and regulatory constraints as product inputs rather than blockers. MLR-approved-content-only on MCE is the same instinct PCI-DSS and SCA demand here.</li><li data-b="c9-33"><strong>Model-adjacent rigour</strong> — You are used to evidence-driven decisions on model-adjacent products, including owning quality thresholds and arguing about what ‘good enough to ship’ means.</li><li data-b="c9-34"><strong>Multi-party stakeholder depth</strong> — Pharma’s provider / payer / regulator complexity rhymes closely with the four-party trust dynamic in payments. You have navigated multi-party systems where no one party is in charge.</li></ul><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c9-35">The one gap to name honestly rather than hide: real-time scale and latency. MCE runs batch, generated ahead of time, where latency is not a hard constraint. Payments demands real-time decisions at vastly higher volume. Saying so directly earns more credibility than pretending familiarity you don’t have — and it is consistent with how you have handled gaps throughout your interviews.</div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="9">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-9"></div>
    <div id="yn-9"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch10" data-idx="10">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta"><span class="ch-k">Part 10</span>
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="10"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Culture, Pace, and What Surprises People</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c10-0">Useful expectations-setting from people who have actually worked there or in comparable payments environments.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c10-1"><span class="num">10.1 </span> Mastercard Culture — The Honest Read</h3><p data-b="c10-2">Public employee reviews rate Mastercard well overall (roughly <span class="num">4.1</span>/<span class="num">5,</span> with strong work-life balance and benefits), but the criticisms are consistent enough to plan around:</p><ul><li data-b="c10-3"><strong>Bureaucracy</strong> — Approvals, reviews, stakeholder alignment, and brand/legal/compliance sign-off make even simple things feel heavy.</li><li data-b="c10-4"><strong>Highly matrixed</strong> — Ownership gets blurry — many teams have input, but not everyone has clear decision rights. Identify who actually decides, early.</li><li data-b="c10-5"><strong>Pace</strong> — Innovation moves more slowly than the brand suggests; priorities are funding-gated and promotion cycles are slow.</li><li data-b="c10-6"><strong>Progression</strong> — Widely described as determined by your ability to form strong cross-functional relationships — which is why the relationship backlog in Part <span class="num">8</span> matters as much as the learning backlog.</li></ul><p data-b="c10-7">None of this is disqualifying — it is just the operating environment. Plan for a longer runway on decisions than you had at ZS, and invest disproportionately in relationships early.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c10-8"><span class="num">10.2 </span> What Surprises PMs New to Payments</h3><blockquote class="q" data-b="c10-9"><em>“When I started working on payment systems at scale, I thought: ‘This is boring infrastructure work. Not real product management.’ I was wrong.”</em>   — A payments PM, on ramping into the domain</blockquote><ul><li data-b="c10-10"><strong><span class="num">99.99%</span> is the bar</strong> — Not <span class="num">99.9%</span>. At scale, <span class="num">99.9%</span> uptime means about <span class="num">43</span> minutes of downtime a month — and during a peak shopping period, every minute is enormous revenue.</li><li data-b="c10-11"><strong>Silent revenue leaks</strong> — A meaningful share of legitimate transactions fail silently, and a chunk of those are the company’s own fraud false positives. Finding and fixing them (smart retries, card updater, better fraud rules, inline 3DS) is real, measurable product value.</li><li data-b="c10-12"><strong>Compliance shapes UX</strong> — PCI, SCA, and KYC shape the user experience and the conversion rate directly. They are not someone else’s problem to handle at the end.</li></ul><h3 class="sec" data-b="c10-13"><span class="num">10.3 </span> Tempered Expectations on Agentic Commerce</h3><p data-b="c10-14">Worth holding internally so you sound measured rather than hype-driven. Mastercard’s own Chief Product Officer, on Agent Pay for Machines, publicly framed it as a long-horizon bet rather than an imminent revenue driver — a meaningful new addressable market over roughly five years, not next year. Agentic payment volumes today remain a small fraction of broader commercial flows.</p><p data-b="c10-15">There is also a real cautionary datapoint from the field: in an early in-chat checkout pilot, conversion was reported as substantially lower when purchases completed inside the AI assistant than when users were redirected to the retailer’s own site. The technology working is not the same as the experience converting — a distinctly product problem, and exactly the kind of thing you should be alert to.</p><aside class="c c-pm"><span class="c-l no-ann">What this means for you</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c10-16"><em>Being the person in the room who is genuinely excited about agentic commerce AND clear-eyed about its current conversion and trust gaps is a strong position. Enthusiasm without skepticism reads as naive; skepticism without enthusiasm reads as the wrong hire. Hold both.</em></div></aside><h3 class="sec" data-b="c10-17"><span class="num">10.4 </span> What’s Confirmed vs. What’s Inferred</h3><p data-b="c10-18">Read this before you repeat anything in this document as fact inside Mastercard.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Claim</th><th class="no-ann">Status</th><th class="no-ann">How to treat it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c10-19">Four-party model, transaction lifecycle, interchange mechanics</td><td data-b="c10-20">Confirmed / industry standard</td><td data-b="c10-21">Use freely</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c10-22">Agent Pay, Agentic Tokens, Acceptance Framework, AP4M, Agent Suite exist</td><td data-b="c10-23">Confirmed via Mastercard announcements</td><td data-b="c10-24">Use freely</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c10-25">DI scores <span class="num">~143B</span> transactions/yr; DI Pro sub-<span class="num">50ms</span>, ~1T data points, <span class="num">20</span>–<span class="num">300%</span> uplift</td><td data-b="c10-26">Mastercard’s own figures</td><td data-b="c10-27">Attribute to Mastercard; don’t present as independently verified</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c10-28">‘Payment Performance Intelligence’ as a team name</td><td data-b="c10-29">NOT found publicly</td><td data-b="c10-30">Confirm the actual charter in onboarding. Closest public analogs are payment-optimization and DI-adjacent product lines.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c10-31">Your specific JD responsibilities</td><td data-b="c10-32">Inferred from adjacent Mastercard agentic-AI roles</td><td data-b="c10-33">Strongly indicative, not identical. Verify with Dhruba.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c10-34">False-decline figures (<span class="num">~$118B</span> vs <span class="num">~$9B</span>)</td><td data-b="c10-35">Directional, dated study</td><td data-b="c10-36">Use as order-of-magnitude, not a current number</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c10-37">Agentic market forecasts</td><td data-b="c10-38">Projections, not results</td><td data-b="c10-39">Never state as fact</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><aside class="c c-key"><span class="c-l no-ann">Key point</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c10-40">The single most important item in that table: nobody outside the team can confirm what ‘Payment Performance Intelligence’ actually covers. Go in with it as a hypothesis — DI/DI Pro-adjacent fraud and payment-optimization intelligence — and confirm it in your first week rather than assuming.</div></aside><aside class="c c-test"><span class="c-l no-ann">Say it out loud</span><div class="c-b" data-b="c10-41">Name three things in this document you would happily assert in a Mastercard meeting, and three you would only say with ‘I read that…’ or would confirm first. If you can’t separate them, reread <span class="num">10.4.</span></div></aside>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="10">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-10"></div>
    <div id="yn-10"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch11" data-idx="11">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta">
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="11"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c11-0">Pull this up mid-meeting if a term flies past you. Skim it once a day this week and it’ll stick.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Term</th><th class="no-ann">Plain-English meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c11-1">PAN</td><td data-b="c11-2">The 16-digit card number (Primary Account Number).</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-3">BIN / IIN</td><td data-b="c11-4">First <span class="num">6</span>–<span class="num">8</span> digits of the card — identifies the issuing bank, used for routing.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-5">CVV / CVC</td><td data-b="c11-6"><span class="num">3</span>–<span class="num">4</span> digit security code proving you hold the card.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-7">EMV</td><td data-b="c11-8">Chip standard that replaced magstripe; one-time cryptogram per transaction.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-9">Issuer</td><td data-b="c11-10">The cardholder’s bank. Holds the money/credit, makes the approve/decline call, bears credit risk.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-11">Acquirer</td><td data-b="c11-12">The merchant’s bank. Processes transactions and pays out the merchant.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-13">Interchange</td><td data-b="c11-14">Fee the merchant’s side pays the issuer on each transaction. Mastercard does NOT keep this.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-15">Scheme / assessment fee</td><td data-b="c11-16">The small cut Mastercard actually keeps for running the network.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-17">MDR</td><td data-b="c11-18">Merchant Discount Rate — total <span class="num">~2</span>–<span class="num">3%</span> the merchant pays, split across issuer, network, acquirer.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-19">Authorization</td><td data-b="c11-20">Real-time approve/decline. Reserves money; doesn’t move it.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-21">Clearing</td><td data-b="c11-22">End-of-day reconciliation of what actually happened.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-23">Settlement</td><td data-b="c11-24">The actual movement of money between banks (usually <span class="num">T+1</span>/<span class="num">T+2</span>).</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-25">Chargeback</td><td data-b="c11-26">Forced reversal of a transaction, initiated by the issuer after a dispute.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-27">AVS</td><td data-b="c11-28">Address Verification Service — checks billing address for card-not-present transactions.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-29">3DS</td><td data-b="c11-30">3-D Secure — the OTP/biometric step-up layer for risky online payments.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-31">ISO <span class="num">8583</span></td><td data-b="c11-32">Old, fixed-format transaction messaging standard.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-33">ISO <span class="num">20022</span></td><td data-b="c11-34">New, rich, structured messaging standard the industry is migrating to.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-35">PCI-DSS</td><td data-b="c11-36">Security standard for anyone handling card data. (The HIPAA of payments.)</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-37">KYC / AML</td><td data-b="c11-38">Know Your Customer / Anti-Money-Laundering — identity and source-of-funds checks.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-39">Tokenization / MDES</td><td data-b="c11-40">Replacing the real card number with a scoped, device-bound token. MDES is Mastercard’s platform.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-41">CNP fraud</td><td data-b="c11-42">Card-Not-Present fraud — online/phone, no physical card.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-43">ATO</td><td data-b="c11-44">Account Takeover — fraudster gets into a genuine account.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-45">Precision</td><td data-b="c11-46">Of everything flagged as fraud, the share that truly was fraud.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-47">Recall</td><td data-b="c11-48">Of all actual fraud, the share that got caught.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-49">DI / DI Pro</td><td data-b="c11-50">Mastercard’s real-time fraud-scoring engine / its gen-AI-enhanced successor.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-51">Brighterion</td><td data-b="c11-52">AI fraud-scoring firm Mastercard acquired in <span class="num">2017</span>; core to its fraud AI.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-53">Agent Pay / AP4M</td><td data-b="c11-54">Mastercard’s frameworks for AI agents (<span class="num">2025</span>) and machine-to-machine payments (<span class="num">2026</span>).</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-55">Agentic token</td><td data-b="c11-56">A scoped credential defining what an AI agent may buy, how much, and where.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-57">Open banking</td><td data-b="c11-58">Banks sharing data via APIs with consent, enabling new products.</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c11-59">FedNow / RTP</td><td data-b="c11-60">Real-time bank-to-bank payment rails.</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="11">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-11"></div>
    <div id="yn-11"></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="ch" id="ch12" data-idx="12">
  <header class="ch-h">
    <div class="ch-meta">
      <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="12"><span>Read</span></label>
    </div>
    <h2>Progress Tracker</h2>
  </header>
  <p data-b="c12-0">Same pattern as your Copilot training doc. Update the status column as you go, and only tick a self-test once you’ve passed it out loud, no notes.</p><div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th class="no-ann">Part</th><th class="no-ann">What it covers</th><th class="no-ann">Status</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td data-b="c12-1">Part <span class="num">0</span></td><td data-b="c12-2">The one mental shift — network, not bank</td><td data-b="c12-3">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-4">Part <span class="num">1</span></td><td data-b="c12-5">The plumbing — four-party model, lifecycle, economics, idempotency</td><td data-b="c12-6">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-7">Part <span class="num">2</span></td><td data-b="c12-8">Fraud, risk &amp; payment performance — precision/recall, auth levers, scenarios</td><td data-b="c12-9">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-10">Part <span class="num">3</span></td><td data-b="c12-11">Mastercard the company — model, DI/DI Pro, tokenization, competitors, your team</td><td data-b="c12-12">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-13">Part <span class="num">4</span></td><td data-b="c12-14">Agentic AI in payments — Agent Pay, agentic fraud, your bridge</td><td data-b="c12-15">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-16">Part <span class="num">5</span></td><td data-b="c12-17">Trends, standards &amp; KPIs</td><td data-b="c12-18">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-19">Part <span class="num">6</span></td><td data-b="c12-20">The actual problem — agents vs. fraud rules, false-decline economics</td><td data-b="c12-21">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-22">Part <span class="num">7</span></td><td data-b="c12-23">What you own — evals, autonomy ladder, guardrails, observability</td><td data-b="c12-24">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-25">Part <span class="num">8</span></td><td data-b="c12-26">First <span class="num">90</span> days — discovery, quick win, trust</td><td data-b="c12-27">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-28">Part <span class="num">9</span></td><td data-b="c12-29">Stakeholders, influence, and your healthcare bridge</td><td data-b="c12-30">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-31">Part <span class="num">10</span></td><td data-b="c12-32">Culture, pace, confirmed vs. inferred</td><td data-b="c12-33">Not started</td></tr><tr><td data-b="c12-34">Self-tests</td><td data-b="c12-35">All nine passed out loud, no notes</td><td data-b="c12-36">Not started</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div><h3 class="sec" data-b="c12-37">Before you leave ZS</h3><p data-b="c12-38">Two artefacts to get your hands on while you still have access (see Part <span class="num">7.5</span>): a real eval / golden-example spreadsheet, and a real requirements doc for an agent feature. Ask to look at them, not just hear about them.</p><h3 class="sec" data-b="c12-39">Confirm in week one</h3><p data-b="c12-40">What ‘Payment Performance Intelligence’ actually covers, the current authorization-rate and false-decline benchmarks, and who owns the eval and guardrail framework today. Everything else in this document is preparation; those three are the facts only Mastercard can give you.</p>
  <div class="ynotes no-ann">
    <div class="ynotes-h">
      <span>Your notes on this part</span>
      <button class="btn" data-addnote="12">Add a note</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ynadd-12"></div>
    <div id="yn-12"></div>
  </div>
</section>

  <p class="nores" id="nores">Nothing matches that. Try a shorter word.</p>
 </div>
</main>

<button class="top" id="top" aria-label="Back to top">&#8593;</button>
<script>window.PARTS=[{"i":0,"k":"Part 0","t":"The One Mental Shift That Makes Everything Click"},{"i":1,"k":"Part 1","t":"How a Payment Actually Works (The Plumbing)"},{"i":2,"k":"Part 2","t":"Fraud, Risk & Payment Performance (Your Team’s World)"},{"i":3,"k":"Part 3","t":"Mastercard the Company, DI Pro, and Your Likely Team"},{"i":4,"k":"Part 4","t":"Agentic AI in Payments (Why They Hired You)"},{"i":5,"k":"Part 5","t":"Trends, Standards & Key Metrics"},{"i":6,"k":"Part 6","t":"The Actual Problem Your Team Is Solving"},{"i":7,"k":"Part 7","t":"What You Actually Own as an Agentic AI PM"},{"i":8,"k":"Part 8","t":"Your First 90 Days"},{"i":9,"k":"Part 9","t":"Stakeholders and Influence"},{"i":10,"k":"Part 10","t":"Culture, Pace, and What Surprises People"},{"i":11,"k":"","t":"Quick-Reference Glossary"},{"i":12,"k":"","t":"Progress Tracker"}];</script>
<script>(function () {
  "use strict";

  /* ---------------- storage ---------------- */
  var KEY = "mcguide:v2";
  var OLD = "mcguide:read";
  var db = { read: {}, hl: [], notes: {}, ws: [], scratch: "" };
  var ok = true;

  // Allowlist-sanitize the notes-pad HTML. It round-trips through an unauthenticated
  // shared endpoint (any device can push a payload), so never trust it verbatim.
  var NP_ALLOWED = { B: 1, STRONG: 1, I: 1, EM: 1, U: 1, UL: 1, OL: 1, LI: 1, BR: 1, DIV: 1, P: 1, SPAN: 1, INPUT: 1 };
  function sanitizeNote(html) {
    var doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(String(html || ""), "text/html");
    (function clean(node) {
      [].slice.call(node.childNodes).forEach(function (n) {
        if (n.nodeType === 1) {
          if (!NP_ALLOWED[n.tagName]) {
            var frag = doc.createDocumentFragment();
            while (n.firstChild) frag.appendChild(n.firstChild);
            node.replaceChild(frag, n);
            clean(node);
            return;
          }
          if (n.tagName === "INPUT") {
            // only a plain checkbox marker is allowed through; anything else (text
            // inputs, event-handler attributes, etc.) is dropped, not sanitized-in-place
            if (n.getAttribute("type") !== "checkbox") { node.removeChild(n); return; }
            var wasChecked = n.hasAttribute("checked");
            [].slice.call(n.attributes).forEach(function (a) { n.removeAttribute(a.name); });
            n.setAttribute("type", "checkbox");
            n.setAttribute("contenteditable", "false");
            if (wasChecked) n.setAttribute("checked", "");
            return;
          }
          var keepClass = (n.tagName === "UL" && n.getAttribute("class") === "np-todo") ||
            (n.tagName === "LI" && n.getAttribute("class") === "np-done") ? n.getAttribute("class") : null;
          // a per-run font-size (px only, strictly validated) is the one style declaration allowed through
          var styleVal = n.getAttribute("style");
          var fsMatch = n.tagName === "SPAN" && styleVal ? styleVal.trim().match(/^font-size:\s*(\d{1,3})px;?\s*$/) : null;
          var keepStyle = fsMatch ? "font-size: " + fsMatch[1] + "px" : null;
          [].slice.call(n.attributes).forEach(function (a) { n.removeAttribute(a.name); });
          if (keepClass) n.setAttribute("class", keepClass);
          if (keepStyle) n.setAttribute("style", keepStyle);
          clean(n);
        } else if (n.nodeType !== 3) {
          node.removeChild(n);
        }
      });
    })(doc.body);
    return doc.body.innerHTML;
  }

  function load() {
    try {
      var raw = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
      if (raw) {
        var p = JSON.parse(raw);
        db.read = p.read || {};
        db.hl = p.hl || [];
        db.notes = p.notes || {};
        db.ws = p.ws || [];
        db.scratch = sanitizeNote(p.scratch || "");
      } else {
        var o = localStorage.getItem(OLD);
        if (o) db.read = JSON.parse(o);
      }
    } catch (e) { ok = false; }
  }
  var cloudPush = function () {};   // wired up by the sync layer once it's ready
  function saveLocal() {
    if (!ok) return;
    try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(db)); }
    catch (e) { ok = false; if (!cloudLive) toast("Storage is full or blocked. Export your notes to be safe."); }
  }
  function save() { saveLocal(); cloudPush(); }
  var cloudLive = false;
  load();

  var PARTS = window.PARTS || [];

  var uid = function () {
    return Date.now().toString(36) + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 7);
  };
  var $ = function (s, r) { return (r || document).querySelector(s); };
  var $$ = function (s, r) { return [].slice.call((r || document).querySelectorAll(s)); };

  function esc(s) {
    return String(s).replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");
  }
  var toastEl;
  function toast(msg) {
    if (!toastEl) { toastEl = document.createElement("div"); toastEl.className = "toast"; document.body.appendChild(toastEl); }
    toastEl.textContent = msg;
    toastEl.classList.add("on");
    clearTimeout(toastEl._t);
    toastEl._t = setTimeout(function () { toastEl.classList.remove("on"); }, 2600);
  }

  /* ---------------- markdown-lite for user notes ---------------- */
  function mdlite(src) {
    var h = esc(src);
    h = h.replace(/^### (.*)$/gm, "<h5>$1</h5>");
    h = h.replace(/^## (.*)$/gm, "<h4>$1</h4>");
    h = h.replace(/\*\*(.+?)\*\*/g, "<strong>$1</strong>");
    h = h.replace(/(^|[^*])\*([^*\n]+)\*/g, "$1<em>$2</em>");
    h = h.replace(/__(.+?)__/g, "<u>$1</u>");
    h = h.replace(/\[(.+?)\]\((https?:[^)\s]+)\)/g, '<a href="$2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">$1</a>');
    h = h.replace(/(https?:\/\/[^\s<]+)/g, function (m) {
      return m.indexOf('href="') > -1 ? m : '<a href="' + m + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">' + m + "</a>";
    });
    h = h.replace(/(?:^|\n)((?:[-*] .*(?:\n|$))+)/g, function (m, blk) {
      var li = blk.trim().split("\n").map(function (l) {
        return "<li>" + l.replace(/^[-*] /, "") + "</li>";
      }).join("");
      return "\n<ul>" + li + "</ul>\n";
    });
    return h.split(/\n{2,}/).map(function (p) {
      var t = p.trim();
      if (!t) return "";
      if (/^<(h4|h5|ul)/.test(t)) return t;
      return "<p>" + t.replace(/\n/g, "<br>") + "</p>";
    }).join("");
  }

  function inlineCode(s) { return s.replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, "<code>$1</code>"); }

  /* ---------------- text offsets within a block ---------------- */
  function tnodes(el) {
    var w = document.createTreeWalker(el, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT, {
      acceptNode: function (n) {
        if (!n.nodeValue || !n.nodeValue.length) return NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT;
        var p = n.parentElement;
        while (p && p !== el) {
          if (p.classList && p.classList.contains("no-ann")) return NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT;
          p = p.parentElement;
        }
        return NodeFilter.FILTER_ACCEPT;
      }
    });
    var a = [], n;
    while ((n = w.nextNode())) a.push(n);
    return a;
  }
  function blockText(el) {
    return tnodes(el).map(function (n) { return n.nodeValue; }).join("");
  }
  function offsetOf(block, node, off) {
    var ns = tnodes(block), acc = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < ns.length; i++) {
      if (ns[i] === node) return acc + off;
      acc += ns[i].nodeValue.length;
    }
    return -1;
  }
  function wrapRange(block, start, end, cls, id) {
    var ns = tnodes(block), acc = 0, pieces = [];
    for (var i = 0; i < ns.length; i++) {
      var n = ns[i], len = n.nodeValue.length, s = acc, e = acc + len;
      if (e > start && s < end) pieces.push([n, Math.max(0, start - s), Math.min(len, end - s)]);
      acc = e;
      if (acc >= end) break;
    }
    var made = [];
    pieces.forEach(function (p) {
      var node = p[0], a = p[1], b = p[2];
      if (a > 0) node = node.splitText(a);
      if (b - a < node.nodeValue.length) node.splitText(b - a);
      var m = document.createElement("mark");
      m.className = "hl " + cls;
      m.setAttribute("data-hl", id);
      node.parentNode.replaceChild(m, node);
      m.appendChild(node);
      made.push(m);
    });
    return made;
  }

  /* ---------------- highlights ---------------- */
  function paintHl(h) {
    var block = document.querySelector('[data-b="' + h.b + '"]');
    if (!block) return false;
    var txt = blockText(block);
    var s = h.s, e = h.e;
    if (txt.slice(s, e) !== h.t) {          // content shifted; re-anchor by text
      var idx = txt.indexOf(h.t);
      if (idx < 0) return false;
      s = idx; e = idx + h.t.length;
      h.s = s; h.e = e;
    }
    var made = wrapRange(block, s, e, h.c, h.id);
    if (h.n) made.forEach(function (m) { m.classList.add("has-n"); });
    return made.length > 0;
  }
  function paintAll() {
    var lost = 0;
    db.hl.forEach(function (h) { if (!paintHl(h)) lost++; });
    if (lost) toast(lost + " highlight" + (lost > 1 ? "s" : "") + " could not be placed — the text may have changed.");
  }
  function unpaint(id) {
    $$('mark[data-hl="' + id + '"]').forEach(function (m) {
      var p = m.parentNode;
      while (m.firstChild) p.insertBefore(m.firstChild, m);
      p.removeChild(m);
      p.normalize();
    });
  }

  /* ---------------- highlight toolbar ---------------- */
  var htb = $("#htb"), hcb = $("#hcb"), hlt = $("#hlt"), pendingRange = null, activeId = null, hcbEditing = false;

  function hideHtb() { htb.classList.remove("on", "picking"); pendingRange = null; document.body.classList.remove("htb-open"); }
  function hideHlt() { hlt.classList.remove("on"); }
  function showHlt(mark) {
    var h = db.hl.filter(function (x) { return x.id === mark.getAttribute("data-hl"); })[0];
    if (!h || !h.n || document.body.classList.contains("htb-open")) return;
    hlt.textContent = h.n;
    var rect = mark.getBoundingClientRect();
    hlt.style.visibility = "hidden";
    hlt.classList.add("on");
    var w = hlt.offsetWidth, hgt = hlt.offsetHeight;
    var L = Math.max(10, Math.min(window.scrollX + rect.left + rect.width / 2 - w / 2, window.scrollX + document.documentElement.clientWidth - w - 12));
    var T = window.scrollY + rect.top - hgt - 8;
    if (T < window.scrollY + 6) T = window.scrollY + rect.bottom + 8;
    hlt.style.left = L + "px";
    hlt.style.top = T + "px";
    hlt.style.visibility = "";
  }
  document.addEventListener("mouseover", function (ev) {
    var m = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest("mark.hl.has-n");
    if (m) showHlt(m);
  });
  document.addEventListener("mouseout", function (ev) {
    var m = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest("mark.hl.has-n");
    if (m) hideHlt();
  });
  function hideHcb() { hcb.classList.remove("on"); hcbEditing = false; }
  function closeAll() { hideHtb(); hideHcb(); activeId = null; }

  function place(el, rect) {
    var top = window.scrollY + rect.top;
    var left = window.scrollX + rect.left + rect.width / 2;
    el.style.visibility = "hidden";
    el.classList.add("on");
    document.body.classList.add("htb-open");
    hideHlt();
    var w = el.offsetWidth, hgt = el.offsetHeight;
    var L = Math.max(10, Math.min(left - w / 2, window.scrollX + document.documentElement.clientWidth - w - 12));
    var T = top - hgt - 9;
    if (T < window.scrollY + 6) T = window.scrollY + rect.height + rect.top + 9;
    el.style.left = L + "px";
    el.style.top = T + "px";
    el.style.visibility = "";
  }
  function placeSide(el, rect) {
    el.style.visibility = "hidden";
    el.classList.add("on");
    var w = el.offsetWidth, hgt = el.offsetHeight;
    var vw = document.documentElement.clientWidth, gap = 12;
    var rightX = window.scrollX + rect.right + gap;
    var L;
    if (rightX + w + 12 <= window.scrollX + vw) {
      L = rightX;
    } else {
      var leftX = window.scrollX + rect.left - gap - w;
      L = leftX >= window.scrollX + 12 ? leftX : Math.max(10, Math.min(window.scrollX + rect.left, window.scrollX + vw - w - 12));
    }
    var T = window.scrollY + rect.top;
    var maxT = window.scrollY + window.innerHeight - hgt - 12;
    if (T > maxT) T = Math.max(window.scrollY + 12, maxT);
    el.style.left = L + "px";
    el.style.top = T + "px";
    el.style.visibility = "";
  }

  document.addEventListener("mouseup", function (ev) {
    if (pathHits(ev, "htb") || pathHits(ev, "hcb")) return;
    setTimeout(function () {
      var sel = window.getSelection();
      if (!sel || sel.isCollapsed || !sel.rangeCount) { if (htb.classList.contains("picking")) hideHtb(); return; }
      var r = sel.getRangeAt(0);
      var b1 = r.startContainer.parentElement && r.startContainer.parentElement.closest("[data-b]");
      var b2 = r.endContainer.parentElement && r.endContainer.parentElement.closest("[data-b]");
      if (!b1 || b1 !== b2) { hideHtb(); return; }
      var text = sel.toString().trim();
      if (text.length < 2) { hideHtb(); return; }
      var s = offsetOf(b1, r.startContainer, r.startOffset);
      var e = offsetOf(b1, r.endContainer, r.endOffset);
      if (s < 0 || e < 0 || e <= s) { hideHtb(); return; }
      pendingRange = { b: b1.getAttribute("data-b"), s: s, e: e, t: blockText(b1).slice(s, e) };
      activeId = null;
      hideHcb();
      htb.classList.add("picking");
      place(htb, r.getBoundingClientRect());
    }, 10);
  });

  $$("#htb-pick .sw").forEach(function (btn) {
    btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      if (!pendingRange) return;
      makeHl(btn.getAttribute("data-c"), false);
    });
  });
  $("#htb-newcomment").addEventListener("click", function () {
    if (!pendingRange) return;
    makeHl("h-y", true);
  });

  function makeHl(color, openNote) {
    var h = { id: uid(), b: pendingRange.b, s: pendingRange.s, e: pendingRange.e, t: pendingRange.t, c: color, n: "", ts: Date.now() };
    db.hl.push(h);
    save();
    paintHl(h);
    window.getSelection().removeAllRanges();
    hideHtb();
    renderDrawer();
    var m = $('mark[data-hl="' + h.id + '"]');
    if (m) {
      activeId = h.id;
      htb.classList.remove("picking");
      renderHtbDot(h.c);
      place(htb, m.getBoundingClientRect());
      if (openNote) openComment(h, m.getBoundingClientRect());
    }
  }

  /* ---------------- existing highlight: toolbar + comment box ---------------- */
  function renderHtbDot(color) {
    $("#htb-recolor").innerHTML = '<button class="htb-dot ' + color + '" id="htb-dot" title="Change color" aria-label="Change highlight color"></button>';
  }
  function fmtTime(ts) {
    if (!ts) return "";
    return new Date(ts).toLocaleTimeString([], { hour: "numeric", minute: "2-digit" });
  }
  function renderHcbNote(h) {
    var host = $("#hcb-note");
    if (hcbEditing) {
      host.innerHTML =
        '<textarea class="hcb-ta" id="hcb-t" placeholder="Write a note…">' + esc(h.n || "") + "</textarea>" +
        '<div class="hcb-a"><button class="btn" id="hcb-cancel">Cancel</button>' +
        '<button class="btn btn-p" id="hcb-post">' + (h.n ? "Save" : "Post") + "</button></div>";
    } else {
      host.innerHTML =
        '<div class="hcb-body">' + esc(h.n) + "</div>" +
        '<div class="hcb-meta"><span class="hcb-time">' + esc(fmtTime(h.nts)) + '</span>' +
        '<button class="hcb-link" id="hcb-edit-btn">Edit</button></div>';
    }
  }
  function openComment(h, rect) {
    hcbEditing = !h.n;
    $("#hcb-q").textContent = h.t;
    renderHcbNote(h);
    placeSide(hcb, rect);
    if (hcbEditing) setTimeout(function () { var t = $("#hcb-t"); if (t) t.focus(); }, 30);
  }
  function openHtbFor(id, rect) {
    var h = db.hl.filter(function (x) { return x.id === id; })[0];
    if (!h) return;
    activeId = id;
    hideHcb();
    htb.classList.remove("picking");
    renderHtbDot(h.c);
    place(htb, rect);
  }

  // A click inside #htb/#hcb can trigger a delegated handler that replaces its own
  // innerHTML (recolor swatches, edit/cancel/post) before this listener runs, detaching
  // ev.target from the live tree — so ev.target.closest() would wrongly read as "outside".
  // composedPath() is captured at dispatch time and stays accurate regardless of that mutation.
  function pathHits(ev, id) {
    var el = document.getElementById(id);
    return !!(ev.composedPath && el && ev.composedPath().indexOf(el) > -1);
  }
  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var m = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest("mark.hl");
    if (m) { openHtbFor(m.getAttribute("data-hl"), m.getBoundingClientRect()); return; }
    if (pathHits(ev, "htb") || pathHits(ev, "hcb")) return;
    closeAll();
  });

  $("#htb-comment").addEventListener("click", function () {
    var h = db.hl.filter(function (x) { return x.id === activeId; })[0];
    if (!h) return;
    var mk = $('mark[data-hl="' + activeId + '"]');
    openComment(h, mk ? mk.getBoundingClientRect() : htb.getBoundingClientRect());
  });
  $("#htb-del").addEventListener("click", function () {
    var i = db.hl.findIndex(function (x) { return x.id === activeId; });
    if (i < 0) return;
    unpaint(activeId);
    db.hl.splice(i, 1);
    save();
    closeAll();
    renderDrawer();
  });
  $("#htb-recolor").addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var h = db.hl.filter(function (x) { return x.id === activeId; })[0];
    if (!h) return;
    if (ev.target.closest("#htb-dot")) {
      $("#htb-recolor").innerHTML =
        '<button class="sw sw-y" data-c="h-y" aria-label="Yellow"></button>' +
        '<button class="sw sw-g" data-c="h-g" aria-label="Green"></button>' +
        '<button class="sw sw-b" data-c="h-b" aria-label="Blue"></button>' +
        '<button class="sw sw-p" data-c="h-p" aria-label="Purple"></button>';
      return;
    }
    var sw = ev.target.closest(".sw");
    if (sw) {
      h.c = sw.getAttribute("data-c");
      save();
      $$('mark[data-hl="' + h.id + '"]').forEach(function (m) { m.className = "hl " + h.c + (h.n ? " has-n" : ""); });
      renderHtbDot(h.c);
      renderDrawer();
    }
  });
  $("#hcb-note").addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var h = db.hl.filter(function (x) { return x.id === activeId; })[0];
    if (!h) return;
    if (ev.target.closest("#hcb-edit-btn")) {
      hcbEditing = true;
      renderHcbNote(h);
      setTimeout(function () { var t = $("#hcb-t"); if (t) { t.focus(); t.setSelectionRange(t.value.length, t.value.length); } }, 10);
      return;
    }
    if (ev.target.closest("#hcb-cancel")) {
      if (!h.n) hideHcb();
      else { hcbEditing = false; renderHcbNote(h); }
      return;
    }
    if (ev.target.closest("#hcb-post")) {
      h.n = $("#hcb-t").value.trim();
      h.nts = Date.now();
      save();
      $$('mark[data-hl="' + h.id + '"]').forEach(function (m) { m.classList.toggle("has-n", !!h.n); });
      renderDrawer();
      if (!h.n) hideHcb();
      else { hcbEditing = false; renderHcbNote(h); }
      toast(h.n ? "Note saved" : "Note cleared");
    }
  });

  /* ---------------- per-part notes ---------------- */
  function partNotes(idx) { return db.notes[idx] || (db.notes[idx] = []); }

  function renderPartNotes(idx) {
    var wrap = $('#yn-' + idx);
    if (!wrap) return;
    var list = partNotes(idx);
    wrap.innerHTML = list.map(function (n) {
      return '<div class="yn no-ann" data-n="' + n.id + '">' +
        '<div class="yn-m"><span class="yn-d">' + new Date(n.ts).toLocaleDateString(undefined, { day: "numeric", month: "short", year: "numeric" }) + "</span>" +
        '<span class="yn-x"><button class="pinbtn' + (n.pin ? " on" : "") + '" data-pin="' + n.id + '" data-scope="part">' + (n.pin ? "Pinned" : "Pin") + '</button>' +
        '<button data-edit="' + n.id + '">Edit</button><button data-del="' + n.id + '">Delete</button></span></div>' +
        '<div class="yn-body">' + inlineCode(mdlite(n.body)) + "</div></div>";
    }).join("");
  }

  function editor(initial, onSave, onCancel) {
    var d = document.createElement("div");
    d.className = "no-ann";
    d.innerHTML = '<textarea class="ed" placeholder="Write anything — competitor notes, a link, a question to ask Dhruba. Markdown works: ## heading, **bold**, - bullets, [text](url)."></textarea>' +
      '<div class="ed-a"><button class="btn btn-p" data-a="save">Save</button><button class="btn" data-a="cancel">Cancel</button>' +
      '<span class="ed-h">Markdown supported</span></div>';
    var ta = $("textarea", d);
    ta.value = initial || "";
    $('[data-a="save"]', d).addEventListener("click", function () {
      var v = ta.value.trim();
      if (!v) { toast("Nothing to save"); return; }
      onSave(v);
    });
    $('[data-a="cancel"]', d).addEventListener("click", onCancel);
    setTimeout(function () { ta.focus(); }, 20);
    return d;
  }

  $$("[data-addnote]").forEach(function (btn) {
    btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      var idx = btn.getAttribute("data-addnote");
      var host = $("#ynadd-" + idx);
      if (host.firstChild) { host.innerHTML = ""; return; }
      var ed = editor("", function (v) {
        partNotes(idx).push({ id: uid(), body: v, ts: Date.now() });
        save(); host.innerHTML = ""; renderPartNotes(idx); renderDrawer(); toast("Note added");
      }, function () { host.innerHTML = ""; });
      host.appendChild(ed);
    });
  });

  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var del = ev.target.getAttribute && ev.target.getAttribute("data-del");
    var edt = ev.target.getAttribute && ev.target.getAttribute("data-edit");
    if (!del && !edt) return;
    var card = ev.target.closest("[data-n]");
    var wrap = ev.target.closest("[id^=yn-]");
    if (!wrap) return;
    var idx = wrap.id.slice(3);
    var list = partNotes(idx);
    var i = list.findIndex(function (n) { return n.id === (del || edt); });
    if (i < 0) return;
    if (del) {
      list.splice(i, 1); save(); renderPartNotes(idx); renderDrawer(); return;
    }
    var ed = editor(list[i].body, function (v) {
      list[i].body = v; save(); renderPartNotes(idx); renderDrawer(); toast("Note updated");
    }, function () { renderPartNotes(idx); });
    card.replaceWith(ed);
  });

  /* ---------------- workspace ---------------- */
  function renderWs() {
    var host = $("#ws-list");
    if (!host) return;
    if (!db.ws.length) {
      host.innerHTML = '<p class="ws-empty">Nothing here yet. Add a section for competitor research, product notes, questions for your team — anything you want to keep alongside the guide.</p>';
      return;
    }
    host.innerHTML = db.ws.map(function (s) {
      return '<section class="ws-sec" id="ws-' + s.id + '">' +
        '<div class="ws-h"><h3 data-b="ws-' + s.id + '">' + esc(s.title) + "</h3>" +
        '<span class="yn-x no-ann"><button data-wsadd="' + s.id + '">Add entry</button>' +
        '<button data-wsren="' + s.id + '">Rename</button>' +
        '<button data-wsdel="' + s.id + '">Delete</button></span></div>' +
        '<div id="wsadd-' + s.id + '" class="no-ann"></div>' +
        '<div id="wse-' + s.id + '"></div></section>';
    }).join("");
    db.ws.forEach(function (s) { renderWsEntries(s.id); });
  }
  function renderWsEntries(sid) {
    var s = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === sid; })[0];
    var host = $("#wse-" + sid);
    if (!s || !host) return;
    host.innerHTML = (s.entries || []).map(function (e) {
      return '<div class="yn no-ann" data-we="' + e.id + '">' +
        '<div class="yn-m"><span class="yn-d">' + new Date(e.ts).toLocaleDateString(undefined, { day: "numeric", month: "short", year: "numeric" }) + "</span>" +
        '<span class="yn-x"><button class="pinbtn' + (e.pin ? " on" : "") + '" data-pin="' + e.id + '" data-scope="ws">' + (e.pin ? "Pinned" : "Pin") + '</button>' +
        '<button data-weedit="' + e.id + '" data-s="' + sid + '">Edit</button>' +
        '<button data-wedel="' + e.id + '" data-s="' + sid + '">Delete</button></span></div>' +
        '<div class="yn-body">' + inlineCode(mdlite(e.body)) + "</div></div>";
    }).join("");
  }

  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var t = ev.target;
    var g = function (a) { return t.getAttribute && t.getAttribute(a); };
    var sid;

    if ((sid = g("data-wsadd"))) {
      var host = $("#wsadd-" + sid);
      if (host.firstChild) { host.innerHTML = ""; return; }
      host.appendChild(editor("", function (v) {
        var s = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === sid; })[0];
        s.entries = s.entries || [];
        s.entries.push({ id: uid(), body: v, ts: Date.now() });
        save(); host.innerHTML = ""; renderWsEntries(sid); renderDrawer(); toast("Entry added");
      }, function () { host.innerHTML = ""; }));
      return;
    }
    if ((sid = g("data-wsren"))) {
      var s2 = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === sid; })[0];
      var nt = prompt("Rename section", s2.title);
      if (nt && nt.trim()) { s2.title = nt.trim(); save(); renderWs(); buildNav(); }
      return;
    }
    if ((sid = g("data-wsdel"))) {
      var s3 = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === sid; })[0];
      if (!confirm('Delete "' + s3.title + '" and everything in it?')) return;
      db.ws = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id !== sid; });
      save(); renderWs(); buildNav(); renderDrawer();
      return;
    }
    var eid;
    if ((eid = g("data-wedel"))) {
      var sd = g("data-s");
      var ss = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === sd; })[0];
      ss.entries = ss.entries.filter(function (e) { return e.id !== eid; });
      save(); renderWsEntries(sd); renderDrawer();
      return;
    }
    if ((eid = g("data-weedit"))) {
      var se = g("data-s");
      var sx = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === se; })[0];
      var en = sx.entries.filter(function (e) { return e.id === eid; })[0];
      var card = t.closest("[data-we]");
      card.replaceWith(editor(en.body, function (v) {
        en.body = v; save(); renderWsEntries(se); renderDrawer(); toast("Entry updated");
      }, function () { renderWsEntries(se); }));
      return;
    }
  });

  /* ---------------- home dashboard ---------------- */
  var IC = {
    book: '<path d="M12 6.6v13.2M12 6.6C10.4 5 7.9 4.3 4.4 4.6v12.9c3.5-.3 6 .4 7.6 2M12 6.6c1.6-1.6 4.1-2.3 7.6-2v12.9c-3.5-.3-6 .4-7.6 2"/>',
    check: '<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8.6"/><path d="m8.2 12.3 2.6 2.6 5-5.3"/>',
    doc: '<path d="M13.4 3.4H6.9a1.7 1.7 0 0 0-1.7 1.7v13.8a1.7 1.7 0 0 0 1.7 1.7h10.2a1.7 1.7 0 0 0 1.7-1.7V8.4z"/><path d="M13.4 3.4v5h5.4"/>',
    mark: '<path d="M6.7 3.8h10.6v16.8L12 16.5l-5.3 4.1z"/>',
    pulse: '<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8.6"/><path d="M7.5 12.2h2.2l1.6-3.5 2.1 6.6 1.5-3.1h1.6"/>',
    globe: '<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8.6"/><path d="M3.4 12h17.2M12 3.4a13.4 13.4 0 0 1 0 17.2 13.4 13.4 0 0 1 0-17.2"/>',
    user: '<circle cx="12" cy="8.4" r="3.5"/><path d="M5.2 20.4a6.8 6.8 0 0 1 13.6 0"/>',
    warn: '<path d="M12 4.2 2.9 20.1h18.2z"/><path d="M12 10.2v4M12 17.1v.2"/>',
    box: '<path d="m12 3.3 8.2 4.5v8.4L12 20.7l-8.2-4.5V7.8z"/><path d="m3.8 7.8 8.2 4.5 8.2-4.5M12 12.3v8.4"/>',
    server: '<rect x="3.6" y="4.2" width="16.8" height="6.2" rx="1.7"/><rect x="3.6" y="13.6" width="16.8" height="6.2" rx="1.7"/><path d="M7.2 7.3h.2M7.2 16.7h.2"/>',
    bulb: '<path d="M9.4 17.6h5.2M10.2 20.6h3.6"/><path d="M12 3.4a6.2 6.2 0 0 0-3.5 11.3c.5.4.8 1 .8 1.6v.3h5.4v-.3c0-.6.3-1.2.8-1.6A6.2 6.2 0 0 0 12 3.4z"/>',
    chart: '<path d="M3.8 20.2h16.4"/><path d="M7 17.2v-5.6M12 17.2V6.9M17 17.2v-3.4"/>'
  };
  function icon(name, tone) {
    return '<span class="ico i-' + tone + '"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">' + IC[name] + "</svg></span>";
  }

  var TOPICS = [
    ["Industry landscape", "Understand the payments industry, key players, and market dynamics.", "Competitors, Market size, Major forces", "globe", "blue"],
    ["User personas", "Who are we solving for? Understand users, their needs and behaviours.", "Users, Jobs, Goals, Pain points", "user", "green"],
    ["Problems & opportunities", "Key problems worth solving and the opportunities for impact.", "Problem statements, Insights", "warn", "amber"],
    ["Product details", "Deep dive into products, platforms, features and how they create value.", "Products, Features, Roadmap", "box", "violet"],
    ["Technology & architecture", "Core systems, tech stack, integrations and how everything connects.", "Architecture, APIs, Data flow", "server", "blue"],
    ["Trends & innovations", "Emerging trends shaping the future of payments and Mastercard's bets.", "AI, Tokenization, Real-time payments", "bulb", "green"],
    ["Metrics & KPIs", "Key metrics that drive success for the business and products.", "Adoption, TPV, Auth rate, NPS", "chart", "rose"],
    ["Resources & notes", "Important links, docs, learnings and saved references.", "Links, Docs, Notes, Articles", "mark", "violet"]
  ];

  function allNotes() {
    var out = [];
    Object.keys(db.notes).forEach(function (idx) {
      (db.notes[idx] || []).forEach(function (n) {
        out.push({ kind: "note", id: n.id, host: idx, body: n.body, ts: n.ts, pin: !!n.pin, go: "yn-" + idx });
      });
    });
    db.ws.forEach(function (s) {
      (s.entries || []).forEach(function (e) {
        out.push({ kind: "ws", id: e.id, host: s.id, body: e.body, ts: e.ts, pin: !!e.pin, go: "ws-" + s.id, where: s.title });
      });
    });
    db.hl.forEach(function (h) {
      if (h.n) out.push({ kind: "hl", id: h.id, body: h.n, quote: h.t, ts: h.ts || 0, pin: !!h.pin, go: h.id });
    });
    return out;
  }

  function setView(v) {
    document.body.setAttribute("data-view", v);
    $("#nav-home").classList.toggle("on", v === "home");
    if (v === "home") renderHome();
  }

  function fmtDate(ts) {
    if (!ts) return "";
    return new Date(ts).toLocaleDateString(undefined, { day: "numeric", month: "short" });
  }

  function renderStats() {
    var readN = Object.keys(db.read).filter(function (k) { return db.read[k]; }).length;
    var notes = allNotes().length;
    var status = readN === 0 ? "Not started" : (readN >= boxes.length ? "Complete" : "In progress");
    var cards = [
      [icon("book", "blue"), PARTS.length, "Parts"],
      [icon("check", "green"), readN + " of " + boxes.length, "Parts read"],
      [icon("doc", "violet"), notes, "Notes created"],
      [icon("mark", "amber"), db.hl.length, "Highlights"],
      [icon("pulse", "slate"), status, "Learning status"]
    ];
    $("#stats").innerHTML = cards.map(function (c, i) {
      return '<div class="stat' + (i === 4 ? " s-go" : "") + '">' + c[0] +
        "<div><b>" + c[1] + "</b><span>" + c[2] + "</span></div></div>";
    }).join("");
  }

  function renderRing() {
    var readN = Object.keys(db.read).filter(function (k) { return db.read[k]; }).length;
    var total = boxes.length || 1;
    var C = 2 * Math.PI * 45;
    $("#ring-arc").setAttribute("stroke-dasharray", C.toFixed(1));
    $("#ring-arc").setAttribute("stroke-dashoffset", (C * (1 - readN / total)).toFixed(1));
    $("#ring-pct").textContent = Math.round((readN / total) * 100) + "%";
    $("#ring-legend").innerHTML =
      '<div><i class="rl-a"></i>' + readN + " read</div>" +
      '<div><i class="rl-c"></i>' + (total - readN) + " not started</div>";
    $("#ring-note").textContent = readN === 0
      ? "Start anywhere. Part 0 is the shortest."
      : (readN >= total ? "Every part read. Now drill the self-tests." : "Keep going — you are building strong foundations.");
  }

  function collFor(title) {
    var t = title.toLowerCase();
    return db.ws.filter(function (s) { return s.title.toLowerCase() === t; })[0];
  }

  function renderTopics() {
    var extra = db.ws.filter(function (s) {
      return !TOPICS.some(function (t) { return t[0].toLowerCase() === s.title.toLowerCase(); });
    }).map(function (s) {
      return [s.title, "Your own section.", "", "box", "teal"];
    });
    $("#topics").innerHTML = TOPICS.concat(extra).map(function (t) {
      var c = collFor(t[0]);
      var n = c && c.entries ? c.entries.length : 0;
      return '<button class="tcard" data-topic="' + esc(t[0]) + '">' + icon(t[3], t[4]) +
        '<span class="tc-b"><span class="tc-t">' + esc(t[0]) + "</span>" +
        '<span class="tc-d">' + esc(t[1]) + "</span>" +
        (n ? '<span class="tc-n">' + n + " entr" + (n > 1 ? "ies" : "y") + "</span>"
           : (t[2] ? '<span class="tc-g">' + esc(t[2]) + "</span>" : "")) +
        "</span></button>";
    }).join("");
  }

  function renderHome() {
    renderStats(); renderRing(); renderTopics(); renderRecent();
  }

  /* topic card: open the collection, creating it on first click */
  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var b = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest("[data-topic]");
    if (!b) return;
    var title = b.getAttribute("data-topic");
    var c = collFor(title);
    if (!c) {
      c = { id: uid(), title: title, entries: [] };
      db.ws.push(c); save(); renderWs(); buildNav();
      toast('"' + title + '" started — add your first entry');
    }
    setView("doc");
    setTimeout(function () {
      var el = document.getElementById("ws-" + c.id);
      if (el && el.scrollIntoView) el.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "auto", block: "start" });
    }, 30);
  });

  $$("[data-openall]").forEach(function (b) {
    b.addEventListener("click", function () { openDrawer(); });
  });

  function itemRow(it) {
    var label = it.kind === "note" ? "Note" : (it.kind === "ws" ? "Collection" : "Highlight");
    var cls = it.kind === "note" ? "rb-note" : (it.kind === "ws" ? "rb-ws" : "rb-hl");
    var text = (it.body || it.quote || "").replace(/[#*`>\-]/g, " ").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
    return '<a class="ritem" href="#" data-jump="' + it.go + '" data-k="' + it.kind + '">' +
      '<span class="rt">' + esc(text.slice(0, 110)) + "</span>" +
      '<span class="rbadge ' + cls + '">' + label + "</span>" +
      '<span class="rdate">' + fmtDate(it.ts) + "</span></a>";
  }

  function renderRecent() {
    var items = allNotes().sort(function (a, b) { return b.ts - a.ts; }).slice(0, 5);
    $("#recent").innerHTML = items.length ? items.map(itemRow).join("") :
      '<p class="empty-s">Nothing yet. Highlight something in the guide, or write a note.</p>';
    var pins = allNotes().filter(function (i) { return i.pin; }).sort(function (a, b) { return b.ts - a.ts; });
    $("#pinned").innerHTML = pins.length ? pins.map(itemRow).join("") :
      '<p class="empty-s">No pinned notes yet.<br>Pin anything you want to keep in reach.</p>';
  }

  /* jump from home lists */
  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var a = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest("[data-jump]");
    if (!a) return;
    ev.preventDefault();
    var go = a.getAttribute("data-jump"), k = a.getAttribute("data-k");
    setView("doc");
    setTimeout(function () {
      var el = k === "hl" ? $('mark[data-hl="' + go + '"]') : document.getElementById(go);
      if (!el) { toast("Could not find that"); return; }
      if (el.scrollIntoView) el.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "center" });
      el.style.outline = "2px solid var(--gold)"; el.style.outlineOffset = "3px";
      setTimeout(function () { el.style.outline = ""; }, 1600);
    }, 30);
  });

  /* nav / card routing */
  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var a = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest("a[href^='#']");
    if (!a) return;
    var href = a.getAttribute("href");
    if (href === "#home") { ev.preventDefault(); setView("home"); window.scrollTo({ top: 0 }); return; }
    if (href === "#") return;
    if (document.body.getAttribute("data-view") === "home") {
      var target = document.querySelector(href);
      if (target) {
        ev.preventDefault();
        setView("doc");
        setTimeout(function () { if (target.scrollIntoView) target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "auto", block: "start" }); }, 20);
      }
    }
  });

  /* pinning */
  document.addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var p = ev.target.getAttribute && ev.target.getAttribute("data-pin");
    if (!p) return;
    var scope = ev.target.getAttribute("data-scope");
    var item;
    if (scope === "ws") {
      db.ws.forEach(function (s) {
        (s.entries || []).forEach(function (e) { if (e.id === p) item = e; });
      });
    } else {
      Object.keys(db.notes).forEach(function (k) {
        (db.notes[k] || []).forEach(function (n) { if (n.id === p) item = n; });
      });
    }
    if (!item) return;
    item.pin = !item.pin;
    save();
    ev.target.classList.toggle("on", !!item.pin);
    ev.target.textContent = item.pin ? "Pinned" : "Pin";
    renderRecent();
    toast(item.pin ? "Pinned" : "Unpinned");
  });


  function renderDrawer() {
    var body = $("#dr-b");
    var items = [];
    db.hl.forEach(function (h) {
      var block = document.querySelector('[data-b="' + h.b + '"]');
      var ch = block && block.closest(".ch");
      items.push({
        where: ch ? (ch.querySelector(".ch-k") ? ch.querySelector(".ch-k").textContent : "") + " · " + ch.querySelector("h2").textContent : "elsewhere",
        text: h.t, note: h.n, id: h.id, kind: "hl", ts: h.ts || 0
      });
    });
    Object.keys(db.notes).forEach(function (idx) {
      var ch = document.querySelector('.ch[data-idx="' + idx + '"]');
      (db.notes[idx] || []).forEach(function (n) {
        items.push({
          where: ch ? (ch.querySelector(".ch-k") ? ch.querySelector(".ch-k").textContent : "") + " · " + ch.querySelector("h2").textContent : "note",
          text: n.body.slice(0, 180), note: "", id: "yn-" + idx, kind: "note", ts: n.ts
        });
      });
    });
    db.ws.forEach(function (s) {
      (s.entries || []).forEach(function (e) {
        items.push({ where: "Workspace · " + s.title, text: e.body.slice(0, 180), note: "", id: "ws-" + s.id, kind: "ws", ts: e.ts });
      });
    });

    $("#dr-n").textContent = items.length ? String(items.length) : "";
    if (!items.length) {
      body.innerHTML = '<p class="dr-empty">No highlights or notes yet.<br><br>Select any text in the guide to highlight it or attach a note. They will show up here and stay put next time you open this page.</p>';
      return;
    }
    items.sort(function (a, b) { return b.ts - a.ts; });
    body.innerHTML = items.map(function (i) {
      return '<div class="ai" data-go="' + i.id + '" data-kind="' + i.kind + '">' +
        '<div class="ai-w">' + esc(i.where) + "</div>" +
        '<div class="ai-t">' + esc(i.text) + "</div>" +
        (i.note ? '<div class="ai-n">' + esc(i.note) + "</div>" : "") + "</div>";
    }).join("");
  }
  $("#dr-b").addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var it = ev.target.closest("[data-go]");
    if (!it) return;
    var id = it.getAttribute("data-go"), kind = it.getAttribute("data-kind");
    closeDrawer();
    setView("doc");
    setTimeout(function () {
      var el = kind === "hl" ? $('mark[data-hl="' + id + '"]') : document.getElementById(id);
      if (!el) { toast("Could not find that spot"); return; }
      if (el.scrollIntoView) el.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "center" });
      el.style.transition = "outline .2s";
      el.style.outline = "2px solid var(--gold)";
      el.style.outlineOffset = "3px";
      setTimeout(function () { el.style.outline = ""; }, 1600);
    }, 30);
  });
  function openDrawer() { closeNotes(); renderDrawer(); $("#drawer").classList.add("on"); }
  function closeDrawer() { $("#drawer").classList.remove("on"); }
  $("#open-dr").addEventListener("click", openDrawer);
  $("#dr-close").addEventListener("click", closeDrawer);

  /* ---------------- persistent notes pad ---------------- */
  function paintScratch() {
    var ta = $("#np-t");
    if (document.activeElement === ta) return;
    var clean = sanitizeNote(db.scratch);
    if (ta.innerHTML !== clean) ta.innerHTML = clean;
  }
  function setNotesFull(on) {
    $("#np").classList.toggle("full", on);
    $("#np-bd").classList.toggle("on", on);
    var b = $("#np-expand");
    b.title = on ? "Collapse" : "Expand";
    b.setAttribute("aria-label", on ? "Collapse notes" : "Expand to full screen");
    b.innerHTML = on
      ? '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 3v6H3M15 3v6h6M9 21v-6H3M15 21v-6h6"/></svg>'
      : '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 3H3v6M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M15 21h6v-6"/></svg>';
  }
  function openNotes() {
    closeDrawer();
    paintScratch();
    $("#np").classList.add("on");
    $("#np-open").setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
    document.documentElement.classList.add("np-lock");
  }
  function closeNotes() {
    $("#np").classList.remove("on");
    setNotesFull(false);
    $("#np-open").setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
    document.documentElement.classList.remove("np-lock");
  }
  $("#np-open").addEventListener("click", function () {
    if ($("#np").classList.contains("on")) { closeNotes(); return; }
    openNotes();
  });
  $("#np-close").addEventListener("click", closeNotes);
  $("#np-expand").addEventListener("click", function () { setNotesFull(!$("#np").classList.contains("full")); });
  $("#np-bd").addEventListener("click", closeNotes);
  var npSaveTmr = null;
  function npQueueSave() {
    db.scratch = $("#np-t").innerHTML;
    clearTimeout(npSaveTmr);
    npSaveTmr = setTimeout(save, 400);
  }
  $("#np-t").addEventListener("input", npQueueSave);
  function npCmd(cmd) {
    var ta = $("#np-t");
    ta.focus();
    document.execCommand(cmd, false, null);
    npQueueSave();
  }
  $$(".np-tbb").forEach(function (b) { b.addEventListener("mousedown", function (ev) { ev.preventDefault(); }); });
  $("#np-bold").addEventListener("click", function () { npCmd("bold"); });
  $("#np-ital").addEventListener("click", function () { npCmd("italic"); });
  $("#np-und").addEventListener("click", function () { npCmd("underline"); });
  $("#np-list").addEventListener("click", function () { npCmd("insertUnorderedList"); });
  $("#np-t").addEventListener("keydown", function (ev) {
    if (!(ev.metaKey || ev.ctrlKey)) return;
    var k = ev.key.toLowerCase();
    if (k === "b") { ev.preventDefault(); npCmd("bold"); }
    else if (k === "i") { ev.preventDefault(); npCmd("italic"); }
    else if (k === "u") { ev.preventDefault(); npCmd("underline"); }
  });

  /* text size — applies to the selection (or, with the caret collapsed, to text typed
     next), like OneNote/Word, not a zoom of the whole pad */
  function npFsClamp(v) { return Math.max(8, Math.min(60, Math.round(v))); }
  function npCurrentFontSizePx() {
    var sel = window.getSelection();
    if (!sel.rangeCount) return 15;
    var node = sel.getRangeAt(0).startContainer;
    var el = node.nodeType === 3 ? node.parentElement : node;
    if (!el || !$("#np-t").contains(el)) return 15;
    return npFsClamp(parseFloat(getComputedStyle(el).fontSize) || 15);
  }
  // Clicking into the size input blurs #np-t, and focusing a contenteditable back
  // does not reliably restore its previous selection — so the range is captured on
  // every change and explicitly re-applied before acting on it.
  var npSavedRange = null;
  // If the selection is exactly the full contents of one existing size span, resizing
  // again should adjust that span in place rather than nest a new one inside it. Compared
  // by text content rather than Range boundary points, which proved too fragile across a
  // focus round-trip through the size input (node/offset identity isn't always preserved).
  function npEnclosingSizeSpan(range) {
    var startEl = range.startContainer.nodeType === 3 ? range.startContainer.parentElement : range.startContainer;
    var span = startEl && startEl.closest ? startEl.closest('span[style^="font-size"]') : null;
    if (!span || !$("#np-t").contains(span)) return null;
    var endEl = range.endContainer.nodeType === 3 ? range.endContainer.parentElement : range.endContainer;
    var endSpan = endEl && endEl.closest ? endEl.closest('span[style^="font-size"]') : null;
    if (endSpan !== span) return null;
    if (range.toString() !== span.textContent) return null;
    return span;
  }
  function npApplyFontSize(px) {
    px = npFsClamp(px);
    var ta = $("#np-t");
    ta.focus();
    var sel = window.getSelection();
    if (npSavedRange) { sel.removeAllRanges(); sel.addRange(npSavedRange); }
    if (!sel.rangeCount) return;
    var range = sel.getRangeAt(0);
    if (range.collapsed) {
      // nothing selected: anchor the caret inside a span carrying the new size, so
      // whatever gets typed next inherits it (a zero-width space gives the span
      // real content to hold a caret in)
      var span = document.createElement("span");
      span.style.fontSize = px + "px";
      span.appendChild(document.createTextNode("​"));
      range.insertNode(span);
      var caret = document.createRange();
      caret.setStart(span.firstChild, 1);
      caret.collapse(true);
      sel.removeAllRanges();
      sel.addRange(caret);
    } else {
      var existing = npEnclosingSizeSpan(range);
      var target;
      if (existing) {
        existing.style.fontSize = px + "px";
        target = existing;
      } else {
        // wrap the selected content directly via the Range API instead of the legacy
        // execCommand('fontSize') + <font> tag swap, which left the selection anchored
        // outside the new span after the DOM was mutated, silently breaking a second
        // resize (of the same or already-resized text) right after the first
        var wrap = document.createElement("span");
        wrap.style.fontSize = px + "px";
        wrap.appendChild(range.extractContents());
        range.insertNode(wrap);
        target = wrap;
      }
      var picked = document.createRange();
      picked.selectNodeContents(target);
      sel.removeAllRanges();
      sel.addRange(picked);
    }
    npSavedRange = sel.getRangeAt(0).cloneRange();
    $("#np-fs-input").value = px;
    npQueueSave();
  }
  document.addEventListener("selectionchange", function () {
    var ta = $("#np-t");
    var sel = window.getSelection();
    if (!sel.rangeCount) return;
    var r = sel.getRangeAt(0);
    var el = r.startContainer.nodeType === 3 ? r.startContainer.parentElement : r.startContainer;
    if (!el || !ta.contains(el)) return;
    npSavedRange = r.cloneRange();
    $("#np-fs-input").value = npCurrentFontSizePx();
  });
  $("#np-fs-dec").addEventListener("click", function () { npApplyFontSize(npCurrentFontSizePx() - 1); });
  $("#np-fs-inc").addEventListener("click", function () { npApplyFontSize(npCurrentFontSizePx() + 1); });
  $("#np-fs-input").addEventListener("change", function () {
    var v = parseInt(this.value, 10);
    npApplyFontSize(isNaN(v) ? 15 : v);
  });
  $("#np-fs-input").addEventListener("keydown", function (ev) { if (ev.key === "Enter") { ev.preventDefault(); this.blur(); } });

  /* checklist / to-do items */
  function npNewCheckbox() {
    var cb = document.createElement("input");
    cb.type = "checkbox";
    cb.setAttribute("contenteditable", "false");
    return cb;
  }
  function npLiOf(node) {
    var el = node && node.nodeType === 3 ? node.parentElement : node;
    return el && el.closest ? el.closest("li") : null;
  }
  $("#np-check").addEventListener("click", function () {
    var ta = $("#np-t");
    ta.focus();
    var sel = window.getSelection();
    var li = sel.rangeCount ? npLiOf(sel.getRangeAt(0).startContainer) : null;
    var existingUl = li && li.closest ? li.closest("ul.np-todo") : null;
    if (existingUl) {
      $$("input[type=checkbox]", existingUl).forEach(function (cb) { cb.remove(); });
      existingUl.classList.remove("np-todo");
      document.execCommand("insertUnorderedList", false, null);
    } else {
      document.execCommand("insertUnorderedList", false, null);
      var sel2 = window.getSelection();
      var newLi = sel2.rangeCount ? npLiOf(sel2.getRangeAt(0).startContainer) : null;
      var ul = newLi && newLi.closest ? newLi.closest("ul") : null;
      if (ul) {
        ul.classList.add("np-todo");
        $$("li", ul).forEach(function (item) {
          if (!item.querySelector(':scope > input[type="checkbox"]')) item.insertBefore(npNewCheckbox(), item.firstChild);
        });
      }
    }
    npQueueSave();
  });
  $("#np-t").addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
    var cb = ev.target.closest && ev.target.closest('input[type="checkbox"]');
    if (!cb) return;
    var li = cb.closest("li");
    if (li) li.classList.toggle("np-done", cb.checked);
    // innerHTML serialization (used for saving) reflects the checked ATTRIBUTE, which a
    // user click does not update on its own — only the live checked PROPERTY. Sync it.
    if (cb.checked) cb.setAttribute("checked", ""); else cb.removeAttribute("checked");
    npQueueSave();
  });
  $("#np-t").addEventListener("keydown", function (ev) {
    if (ev.key !== "Enter") return;
    var sel = window.getSelection();
    var li = sel.rangeCount ? npLiOf(sel.getRangeAt(0).startContainer) : null;
    if (!li || !li.closest("ul.np-todo")) return;
    setTimeout(function () {
      var sel2 = window.getSelection();
      var newLi = sel2.rangeCount ? npLiOf(sel2.getRangeAt(0).startContainer) : null;
      if (newLi && newLi.closest("ul.np-todo") && !newLi.querySelector(':scope > input[type="checkbox"]')) {
        newLi.insertBefore(npNewCheckbox(), newLi.firstChild);
      }
    }, 0);
  });

  /* ---------------- progress ---------------- */
  var boxes = $$("[data-done]");
  function paintProgress() {
    var n = 0;
    boxes.forEach(function (b) {
      var i = b.getAttribute("data-done"), on = !!db.read[i];
      b.checked = on; if (on) n++;
      var d = document.querySelector('[data-dot="' + i + '"]');
      if (d) d.classList.toggle("done", on);
    });
    $("#cnt").textContent = n + " of " + boxes.length + " read";
    $("#barfill").style.width = (boxes.length ? (n / boxes.length) * 100 : 0) + "%";
  }
  boxes.forEach(function (b) {
    b.addEventListener("change", function () {
      db.read[b.getAttribute("data-done")] = b.checked;
      save(); paintProgress();
      if (document.body.getAttribute("data-view") === "home") renderHome();
    });
  });

  /* ---------------- nav ---------------- */
  function buildNav() {
    var host = $("#nav-ws");
    if (!db.ws.length) { host.innerHTML = ""; return; }
    host.innerHTML = '<li class="nv-grp">Your sections</li>' + db.ws.map(function (s) {
      return '<li><a href="#ws-' + s.id + '"><span class="nv-n">&#183;</span><span class="nv-t">' + esc(s.title) + "</span></a></li>";
    }).join("");
  }

  var chs = $$(".ch");
  var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (es) {
    es.forEach(function (e) {
      if (e.isIntersecting) {
        var i = e.target.getAttribute("data-idx");
        $$("nav a[data-nav]").forEach(function (a) {
          a.classList.toggle("on", a.getAttribute("data-nav") === i);
        });
      }
    });
  }, { rootMargin: "-15% 0px -75% 0px" });
  chs.forEach(function (c) { io.observe(c); });

  /* ---------------- search ---------------- */
  var inp = $("#q"), nores = $("#nores"), tmr = null;
  function clearMarks(root) {
    $$("mark:not(.hl)", root).forEach(function (m) {
      var p = m.parentNode;
      while (m.firstChild) p.insertBefore(m.firstChild, m);
      p.removeChild(m); p.normalize();
    });
  }
  function markIn(el, re) {
    var w = document.createTreeWalker(el, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT, null);
    var nodes = [], n;
    while ((n = w.nextNode())) if (n.nodeValue.trim()) nodes.push(n);
    nodes.forEach(function (node) {
      re.lastIndex = 0;
      if (!re.test(node.nodeValue)) return;
      re.lastIndex = 0;
      var frag = document.createDocumentFragment(), last = 0, s = node.nodeValue, m;
      while ((m = re.exec(s)) !== null) {
        frag.appendChild(document.createTextNode(s.slice(last, m.index)));
        var mk = document.createElement("mark");
        mk.textContent = m[0];
        frag.appendChild(mk);
        last = m.index + m[0].length;
        if (m.index === re.lastIndex) re.lastIndex++;
      }
      frag.appendChild(document.createTextNode(s.slice(last)));
      node.parentNode.replaceChild(frag, node);
    });
  }
  inp.addEventListener("input", function () {
    clearTimeout(tmr);
    tmr = setTimeout(function () {
      var v = inp.value.trim();
      chs.forEach(clearMarks);
      if (v.length < 2) { document.body.classList.remove("searching"); nores.classList.remove("on"); return; }
      document.body.classList.add("searching");
      if (document.body.getAttribute("data-view") === "home") setView("doc");
      var re = new RegExp(v.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&"), "gi");
      var any = false;
      chs.forEach(function (c) {
        re.lastIndex = 0;
        var hit = re.test(c.textContent);
        re.lastIndex = 0;
        c.classList.toggle("hit", hit);
        if (hit) { any = true; markIn(c, re); }
      });
      nores.classList.toggle("on", !any);
    }, 150);
  });
  document.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
    if (e.key === "/" && document.activeElement !== inp && !/^(INPUT|TEXTAREA)$/.test(document.activeElement.tagName)) {
      e.preventDefault(); inp.focus();
    }
    if (e.key === "Escape") {
      if (hcb.classList.contains("on")) { hideHcb(); return; }
      if (htb.classList.contains("on")) { closeAll(); return; }
      if ($("#np").classList.contains("on")) { closeNotes(); return; }
      if ($("#drawer").classList.contains("on")) { closeDrawer(); return; }
      if (document.activeElement === inp) { inp.value = ""; inp.dispatchEvent(new Event("input")); inp.blur(); }
    }
  });

  /* ---------------- misc ---------------- */
  var top = $("#top");
  window.addEventListener("scroll", function () {
    top.classList.toggle("on", window.scrollY > 700);
    if (htb.classList.contains("on")) hideHtb();
    if (hcb.classList.contains("on")) hideHcb();
    if (hlt.classList.contains("on")) hideHlt();
  }, { passive: true });
  top.addEventListener("click", function () { window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: "smooth" }); });

  /* ---------------- boot ---------------- */
  var localWarn = null;
  if (!ok) {
    localWarn = document.createElement("div");
    localWarn.className = "warn";
    localWarn.textContent = "This browser is blocking local storage, so highlights and notes will not be saved. Private browsing is the usual cause.";
    $(".wrap").insertBefore(localWarn, $(".wrap").firstChild);
  }
  paintAll();
  paintProgress();
  paintScratch();
  Object.keys(db.notes).forEach(function (i) { renderPartNotes(i); });
  renderWs();
  buildNav();
  renderDrawer();
  var deep = location.hash && location.hash !== "#home" && document.querySelector(location.hash);
  setView(deep ? "doc" : "home");
  if (deep) setTimeout(function () { var t = document.querySelector(location.hash); if (t && t.scrollIntoView) t.scrollIntoView(); }, 40);

  /* ---------------- cross-device sync (Vercel + Supabase) ---------------- */
  (function () {
    var API = "/api/state";
    var me = uid();
    var lastSeenTs = 0, pushTmr = null, pollTmr = null;

    function headers(extra) {
      var h = { "Content-Type": "application/json" };
      for (var k in extra) h[k] = extra[k];
      return h;
    }

    function repaint() {
      paintAll();
      paintProgress();
      paintScratch();
      Object.keys(db.notes).forEach(function (i) { renderPartNotes(i); });
      renderWs();
      buildNav();
      renderDrawer();
      if (document.body.getAttribute("data-view") === "home") renderHome();
    }

    // Union by id so a device that made changes offline never loses them on first sync.
    function union(local, remote) {
      var out = remote.slice(), have = {}, extra = false;
      remote.forEach(function (x) { have[x.id] = 1; });
      local.forEach(function (x) { if (!have[x.id]) { out.push(x); extra = true; } });
      return { list: out, extra: extra };
    }

    function mergeFirst(d) {
      var extra = false, m;
      m = union(db.hl, d.hl || []); db.hl = m.list; extra = extra || m.extra;

      var ws = (d.ws || []).map(function (s) { return { id: s.id, title: s.title, entries: (s.entries || []).slice() }; });
      m = union(db.ws, ws); extra = extra || m.extra;
      m.list.forEach(function (s) {
        var mine = db.ws.filter(function (x) { return x.id === s.id; })[0];
        if (!mine) return;
        var e = union(mine.entries || [], s.entries || []);
        s.entries = e.list; extra = extra || e.extra;
      });
      db.ws = m.list;

      var notes = {};
      Object.keys(d.notes || {}).forEach(function (k) { notes[k] = (d.notes[k] || []).slice(); });
      Object.keys(db.notes).forEach(function (k) {
        var n = union(db.notes[k] || [], notes[k] || []);
        notes[k] = n.list; extra = extra || n.extra;
      });
      db.notes = notes;

      var read = {};
      Object.keys(d.read || {}).forEach(function (k) { if (d.read[k]) read[k] = true; });
      Object.keys(db.read).forEach(function (k) { if (db.read[k] && !read[k]) { read[k] = true; extra = true; } });
      db.read = read;

      var remoteScratch = typeof d.scratch === "string" ? sanitizeNote(d.scratch) : "";
      if (remoteScratch) {
        if (db.scratch && db.scratch !== remoteScratch) { db.scratch = remoteScratch + "<div>&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;</div>" + db.scratch; extra = true; }
        else if (!db.scratch) { db.scratch = remoteScratch; }
      } else if (db.scratch) { extra = true; }

      return extra;
    }

    function adopt(d) {
      db.hl.forEach(function (h) { unpaint(h.id); });
      db.read = d.read || {};
      db.hl = d.hl || [];
      db.notes = d.notes || {};
      db.ws = d.ws || [];
      db.scratch = sanitizeNote(d.scratch || "");
    }

    function hasLocal() {
      return db.hl.length || db.ws.length || Object.keys(db.notes).length || Object.keys(db.read).length || !!db.scratch;
    }

    function markLive() {
      cloudLive = true;
      if (localWarn) { localWarn.remove(); localWarn = null; }
    }

    function get() {
      return fetch(API, { headers: headers() }).then(function (r) {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error("http " + r.status);
        return r.json();
      });
    }

    function push() {
      if (!cloudLive) return;
      clearTimeout(pushTmr);
      pushTmr = setTimeout(function () {
        var payload = { read: db.read, hl: db.hl, notes: db.notes, ws: db.ws, scratch: db.scratch, wid: me, ts: Date.now() };
        fetch(API, { method: "POST", headers: headers(), body: JSON.stringify(payload) }).then(function (r) {
          if (r.ok) lastSeenTs = payload.ts;
        })["catch"](function () {
          toast("Could not reach the sync server — this device is still saved.");
        });
      }, 500);
    }

    function poll() {
      get().then(function (d) {
        if (!d || d.wid === me) return;
        if ((d.ts || 0) <= lastSeenTs) return;
        lastSeenTs = d.ts;
        adopt(d);
        repaint();
        saveLocal();
      })["catch"](function () {});
    }

    function start() {
      clearInterval(pollTmr);
      get().then(function (d) {
        markLive();
        cloudPush = push;
        if (!d) {
          lastSeenTs = 0;
          if (hasLocal()) push();
        } else {
          var extra = mergeFirst(d);
          lastSeenTs = d.ts || 0;
          repaint();
          saveLocal();
          if (extra) push();
        }
        pollTmr = setInterval(poll, 20000);
      })["catch"](function () {
        setTimeout(start, 10000);
      });
    }

    start();
  })();
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>